window.LESSON_HTML=["<h2>INTRODUCTION</h2>\n<p>We live in a world that runs on digital technology. From how Nigerian banks process transactions in seconds, to how JAMB conducts computer-based exams, to how teachers send assignments on WhatsApp — digital technology has quietly become part of almost everything we do. Yet many students have never stopped to ask: <em>what exactly is digital technology, and how does it work?</em></p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/img/image1.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\"></p>\n<p>This lesson is designed to answer that question. Whether you are preparing for your JSS 1 Basic Technology exam or simply curious about the world around you, understanding digital technology is no longer optional — it is a life skill. As a JSS 1 student in Nigeria today, you are growing up in an era where knowing how to use and understand technology can open doors to great opportunities in the future.</p>\n<p><strong>Learning Objectives</strong></p>\n<p>By the end of this lesson, students should be able to:</p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p><strong>Define</strong> digital technology in their own words</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Explain</strong> how digital technology works in simple terms</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Identify</strong> at least five examples of digital devices in everyday Nigerian life</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Describe</strong> the importance of digital technology in modern society</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Discuss</strong> both the benefits and the potential risks of digital technology</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Demonstrate</strong> responsible and safe use of digital devices</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h2>MEANING OF DIGITAL TECHNOLOGY</h2>\n<p>The word <em><strong>digital</strong></em> comes from the Latin word <em><strong>digitus</strong></em>, meaning finger. In technology, \"digital\" refers to information that is stored, processed, or communicated using numbers — specifically the numbers <strong>0</strong> and <strong>1</strong>, which computers use as a kind of language called <strong>binary code</strong>.</p>\n<p>So, <em><strong>digital technology is the use of electronic tools, systems, devices, and resources that generate, store, or process data in the form of numbers (0s and 1s)</strong>.</em> In simpler terms, <em><strong>it refers to any technology that uses computers or computer-like systems to work</strong></em>.</p>\n<p>Unlike old analogue technology — such as a traditional clock with moving hands or an old radio that picks up sound waves — digital technology converts information into a code of numbers that machines can understand very quickly and accurately.</p>\n<p><strong>A simple analogy:</strong> Imagine you want to send a message to your friend in another class. You could walk there yourself (analogue — slow, physical), or you could send a text message (digital — fast, uses code). Digital technology works like that fast, coded message.</p>\n<h2>HOW DIGITAL TECHNOLOGY WORKS (SIMPLY EXPLAINED)</h2>\n<p>Every piece of digital information — whether it is a photo, a song, a text message, or a document — is broken down into tiny units of data called <strong>bits</strong>. Eight bits make one <strong>byte</strong>. Thousands of bytes make a <strong>kilobyte (KB)</strong>, millions of bytes make a <strong>megabyte (MB)</strong>, and so on.</p>\n<p>When you take a photo with a phone, the camera does not capture the image the way your eyes see it. Instead, it converts the image into millions of tiny coloured dots called <strong>pixels</strong>, and each pixel is stored as a number. That is how a picture becomes a digital file that can be saved, shared, or edited.</p>\n<p>This process — converting real-world information into numbers — is what makes digital technology so powerful. Numbers can be stored on tiny chips, sent across the internet in seconds, and reproduced perfectly without losing quality.</p>\n<h2>IMPORTANCE OF DIGITAL TECHNOLOGY IN MODERN LIFE</h2>\n<p>Digital technology has changed the world in ways that were unimaginable just a few decades ago. Here are some of the most important ways it affects life in Nigeria and beyond:</p>\n<p><strong>1. Communication Has Become Faster and Cheaper</strong></p>\n<p>Before digital technology, Nigerians who had relatives abroad would wait weeks for a letter to arrive. Today, a video call on WhatsApp or Zoom connects people across continents in real time — for almost no cost. MTN, Airtel, Glo, and 9mobile have all built their communication networks on digital technology.</p>\n<p><strong>2. Education Has Become More Accessible</strong></p>\n<p>The rise of digital technology has made it possible for students in Kebbi State to access the same learning materials as students in Lagos. Platforms like YouTube, Educat, and government e-learning portals are being used to deliver lessons to millions of Nigerian students. During the COVID-19 school closures in 2020, digital technology was the only reason education could continue at all.</p>\n<p><strong>3. Business and Commerce Have Been Transformed</strong></p>\n<p>Today, millions of Nigerians use Flutterwave, Paystack, Opay, and mobile banking apps to send and receive money, pay for goods online, and run small businesses without needing a physical shop. Market women in Onitsha use WhatsApp to take orders. Farmers in Kano use mobile apps to check commodity prices. None of this would be possible without digital technology.</p>\n<p><strong>4. Healthcare Has Improved</strong></p>\n<p>Hospitals and clinics now use digital systems to store patient records, conduct laboratory tests, and even perform surgeries with computer-assisted precision. Telemedicine — seeing a doctor through a phone or computer — is becoming more popular in Nigeria, particularly in rural areas where hospitals are far away.</p>\n<p><strong>5. Government and Public Services Are More Efficient</strong></p>\n<p>The Federal Government of Nigeria has introduced several digital initiatives: the National Identity Number (NIN) system, the Integrated Payroll and Personnel Information System (IPPIS), Bank Verification Number (BVN), and computer-based testing for WAEC and JAMB. All of these depend on digital technology to function.</p>\n<p><strong>6. Entertainment and Media Have Been Revolutionised</strong></p>\n<p>Nollywood movies are now streamed on Netflix, YouTube, and ShowMax. Nigerian music artists like Burna Boy and Davido distribute their music digitally to fans all over the world. Afrobeats would not have its global reach without digital distribution platforms.</p>\n<p><strong>7. It Creates Career Opportunities</strong></p>\n<p>Digital technology has created entirely new careers: software developers, graphic designers, cybersecurity experts, data analysts, digital marketers, and content creators. Many young Nigerians are earning good incomes through these digital skills — some even without a university degree.</p>\n<h2>EXAMPLES OF DIGITAL DEVICES</h2>\n<p>A <strong>digital device</strong> is any device that processes, stores, or transmits digital information. Here are the main categories with real-life Nigerian examples:</p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/img/image2.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\"><strong>1. Computers (Desktop and Laptop)</strong></p>\n<p>Computers are the most well-known digital devices. A <strong>desktop computer</strong> is the type found in computer laboratories in schools and offices — it has a separate monitor, keyboard, and processing unit. A <strong>laptop</strong> combines all these parts into a portable device.</p>\n<p>In Nigeria, students use computers in CBT centres for JAMB exams, office workers use them for typing documents and sending emails, and businesses use them for managing accounts and records.</p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/img/image3.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\"><strong>2. Smartphones</strong></p>\n<p>A smartphone is arguably the most widely used digital device in Nigeria today. It is essentially a powerful computer that fits in your pocket. Smartphones can make calls, browse the internet, take photos and videos, run apps, and handle banking transactions.</p>\n<p>With over <strong>200 million active SIM cards</strong> in Nigeria, smartphones have become the primary way most Nigerians access the internet.</p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/img/image4.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\"><strong>3. Tablets</strong></p>\n<p>Tablets are flat, touchscreen digital devices larger than phones but smaller than laptops. They are popular in homes and schools for reading, watching videos, and browsing. Some Nigerian schools now distribute tablets to students as part of digital learning programmes.</p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/img/image5.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\"><strong>4. Digital Televisions</strong></p>\n<p>Modern televisions are digital devices. Unlike the old analogue TV sets that picked up fuzzy signals from antennas, digital televisions receive clear, high-quality signals. Nigeria switched from analogue to digital broadcasting in 2022, meaning all TV signals are now transmitted digitally across the country.</p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/img/image6.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\"><strong>5. Automated Teller Machines (ATMs)</strong></p>\n<p>The ATM machine you see outside bank branches is a digital device. It connects to a bank's computer network to verify your card, check your balance, and dispense cash — all within seconds. Nigeria currently has thousands of ATMs operated by banks such as First Bank, GTBank, Access Bank, and UBA.</p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/img/image7.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\"><strong>6. Digital Cameras</strong></p>\n<p>Digital cameras capture images and store them as digital files rather than on film. Most smartphones today include high-quality digital cameras built in. Journalists, photographers, and even students use digital cameras to capture images for assignments and creative projects.</p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/img/image8.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\"><strong>7. Printers and Scanners</strong></p>\n<p>A digital printer converts digital files stored on a computer into physical printed documents. A scanner does the opposite — it converts physical documents into digital files that can be stored or sent electronically. These are commonly found in schools, offices, and printing shops (often called \"business centres\") across Nigeria.</p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/img/image9.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\"><strong>8. Smart Wearables</strong></p>\n<p>Smartwatches and fitness trackers are wearable digital devices that monitor your health — tracking steps, heart rate, sleep, and more. While not yet as common in Nigeria as phones and computers, they are growing in popularity, especially among health-conscious young Nigerians.</p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/img/image10.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\"><strong>9. Point of Sale (POS) Machines</strong></p>\n<p>POS machines are small digital devices used to accept card and transfer payments. After the Central Bank of Nigeria's cashless policy and the 2023 cash scarcity, POS agents became extremely important, especially in areas with limited banking access. Today, POS machines are found in almost every neighbourhood in Nigeria.</p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/img/image11.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\"><strong>10. Digital Radio and MP3 Players</strong></p>\n<p>Digital radios receive audio signals transmitted as digital data, producing clearer sound than traditional analogue radios. MP3 players store and play music as digital audio files. While smartphones have largely replaced dedicated MP3 players, digital audio technology still powers music streaming apps like Boomplay and Spotify.</p>\n<h2>ADVANTAGES AND DISADVANTAGES OF DIGITAL TECHNOLOGY</h2>\n<p><strong>Advantages</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>Speed:</strong> Digital devices process and share information in fractions of a second</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Accuracy:</strong> Digital systems reduce human error in banking, medical records, and examinations</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Storage:</strong> Millions of documents, photos, and songs can be stored on a device the size of your fingernail (a memory card or USB drive)</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Accessibility:</strong> People in remote areas can access education, healthcare, and financial services via digital devices</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Connectivity:</strong> Digital technology connects people across Nigeria and the world instantly</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Economic opportunity:</strong> It creates jobs and enables small businesses to reach more customers</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Disadvantages</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>Cybercrime:</strong> Digital technology has also made fraud, hacking, and \"yahoo yahoo\" scams possible</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Health concerns:</strong> Too much screen time can harm eyesight and disrupt sleep patterns</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Dependence:</strong> Many people struggle to function without their phones or the internet</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Privacy risks:</strong> Personal information shared online can be stolen or misused</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Environmental impact:</strong> Electronic waste (e-waste) from discarded devices is a growing environmental problem in Nigeria</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Digital divide:</strong> Not everyone in Nigeria has equal access to digital technology, creating inequality between urban and rural populations</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>PRACTICAL APPLICATIONS IN NIGERIAN DAILY LIFE</h2>\n<p>Here are ways you can see digital technology in action around you:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>At school:</strong> CBT (Computer-Based Testing) for exams, projectors in classrooms, e-learning platforms</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>At home:</strong> Smart TVs, smartphones, routers for WiFi internet access</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>In the market:</strong> POS machines for payments, WhatsApp for business communication</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>At the hospital:</strong> Digital X-rays, electronic patient records, health tracking apps</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>In worship centres:</strong> Digital projectors for displaying lyrics and scriptures, sound systems run by digital mixers</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>On the road:</strong> Digital fuel pumps at petrol stations, GPS navigation apps like Google Maps</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>DIGITAL SAFETY AND RESPONSIBLE USE</h2>\n<p>Learning about digital technology also means learning to use it responsibly. Here are important safety rules you should know:</p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p><strong>Protect your personal information</strong> Never share your phone number, home address, school name, or photos with strangers online. Scammers and cybercriminals often target young people who are careless with their personal information.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Be careful what you post</strong> Anything you post online can be seen by many people and may be very difficult to delete. Always think before you post a photo, comment, or video.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Do not spend too long on screens</strong> Health experts recommend no more than two hours of recreational screen time per day for children. Too much screen time can cause eye strain, headaches, and poor sleep.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Ask an adult if you are unsure</strong> If you receive a message, link, or request that makes you uncomfortable online, do not respond. Instead, tell a parent, teacher, or trusted adult immediately.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Do not use technology to bully others</strong> Cyberbullying — using digital devices to harass, threaten, or embarrass others — is harmful, wrong, and in some cases illegal. Treat others online the same way you would like to be treated.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h2>CLASSROOM AND HOME ACTIVITIES</h2>\n<p><strong>Activity 1: Digital Device Hunt</strong></p>\n<p>Walk around your school or home and list every digital device you can find. Write down the name of each device and what it is used for. Compare your list with a classmate's list.</p>\n<p><strong>Activity 2: Digital vs. Analogue</strong></p>\n<p>Draw a table with two columns labelled \"Digital\" and \"Analogue.\" Sort the following items into the correct column: wall clock, smartphone, thermometer (old glass type), digital thermometer, vinyl record, MP3 file, handwritten letter, email, film camera, digital camera.</p>\n<p><strong>Activity 3: My Digital Day</strong></p>\n<p>Keep a diary for one day and write down every time you (or someone in your household) uses a digital device. At the end of the day, count how many times digital technology was used. Write a short paragraph about what surprised you most.</p>\n<p><strong>Activity 4: Debate — Is Digital Technology More Helpful or Harmful?</strong></p>\n<p>Divide the class into two groups. One group argues that digital technology is more helpful than harmful. The other group argues the opposite. Use facts and examples from this lesson and from your own experience to support your arguments.</p>\n<p><strong>Assessment Questions</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Section A — Objective Questions</strong></p>\n<p>1. Which of the following best describes digital technology?</p>\n<p>A. Technology that uses sound waves to transmit information</p>\n<p>B. Technology that processes information using numbers (0s and 1s)</p>\n<p>C. Technology that only works with physical buttons</p>\n<p>D. Technology invented only in the 21st century</p>\n<p>2. Which of the following is NOT a digital device?</p>\n<p>A. Smartphone</p>\n<p>B. ATM machine</p>\n<p>C. A clock with moving hands (analogue clock)</p>\n<p>D. Digital television</p>\n<p>3. The smallest unit of digital data is called a __________.</p>\n<p>A. Megabyte</p>\n<p>B. Pixel</p>\n<p>C. Bit</p>\n<p>D. Kilobyte</p>\n<p>4. Which of the following is a disadvantage of digital technology?</p>\n<p>A. It makes communication faster</p>\n<p>B. It stores large amounts of information</p>\n<p>C. It can be used to commit cybercrime</p>\n<p>D. It creates job opportunities</p>\n<p>5. A POS machine is used in Nigeria mainly for:</p>\n<p>A. Watching television</p>\n<p>B. Accepting card and transfer payments</p>\n<p>C. Printing photographs</p>\n<p>D. Sending emails</p>\n<p><strong>Section B — Theory Questions</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Define digital technology and explain the difference between digital and analogue technology, using one example of each. <em><strong>(6 marks)</strong></em></p>\n<p><strong>2.</strong> List and explain <strong>four</strong> ways in which digital technology has improved everyday life in Nigeria, giving specific examples for each point. <em><strong>(8 marks)</strong></em></p>\n<p><strong>3.</strong> Why is it important for JSS 1 students to learn about digital safety? Mention <strong>three</strong> digital safety rules and explain why each one matters. <em><strong>(6 marks)</strong></em></p>\n<p><strong>Summary</strong></p>\n<p>In this lesson, we have covered the following key points:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>Digital technology</strong> refers to the use of electronic systems and devices that process, store, and transmit information using numbers (0s and 1s)</p></li>\n<li><p>The basic unit of digital data is a <strong>bit</strong>, and eight bits make one <strong>byte</strong></p></li>\n<li><p>Digital technology is important because it improves <strong>communication, education, business, healthcare</strong>, and <strong>government services</strong> in Nigeria</p></li>\n<li><p>Common <strong>digital devices</strong> include smartphones, computers, ATMs, digital televisions, POS machines, printers, scanners, tablets, digital cameras, and smart wearables</p></li>\n<li><p>Like all powerful tools, digital technology has both <strong>advantages</strong> (speed, accuracy, accessibility) and <strong>disadvantages</strong> (cybercrime, health risks, digital divide)</p></li>\n<li><p>Responsible use of digital technology includes protecting personal information, limiting screen time, and treating others with respect online</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Conclusion</strong></p>\n<p>Digital technology is not just a subject to study for exams — it is the language of the modern world. Every day, in every part of Nigeria, from the ATM on your street to the phone in your hand, digital technology is making life faster, easier, and more connected.</p>\n<p>As a JSS 1 student today, you are not just a user of technology — you could one day be one of the people who builds it. Nigerian tech companies like Andela, Interswitch, and Paystack were built by young Nigerians who once sat in a classroom just like yours and dared to dream big. That journey begins with understanding the basics — and you have just taken your first step.</p>\n<p>Stay curious, stay safe, and keep exploring.</p>\n<p><strong>Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1. What is the simple definition of digital technology for JSS 1 students?</strong> <br />\nDigital technology is the use of electronic devices and systems that work by processing information as numbers (0s and 1s). Examples include smartphones, computers, ATMs, and digital TVs.</p>\n<p><strong>2. Why is digital technology important in Nigeria?</strong> </p>\n<p>Digital technology is important in Nigeria because it has improved communication, made banking more accessible through mobile apps and POS machines, enabled online education, and created jobs in the tech industry. It has also helped the government to manage public records and conduct secure examinations.</p>\n<p><strong>3. What are examples of digital devices students can find at home?</strong> </p>\n<p>Common digital devices found in Nigerian homes include smartphones, televisions (digital), laptops or tablets, home routers (for WiFi), digital radios, and electric meters with digital displays.</p>\n<p><strong>4. What is the difference between digital technology and analogue technology?</strong> </p>\n<p>Analogue technology uses continuous signals to store and transmit information — like the moving hands of a clock or sound waves on a vinyl record. Digital technology converts information into numbers (0s and 1s) before storing or transmitting it. Digital technology is generally faster, more accurate, and easier to store than analogue.</p>\n<p><strong>5. How can JSS 1 students stay safe online?</strong> </p>\n<p>Students should never share personal information with strangers online, avoid clicking on suspicious links, limit screen time, report uncomfortable online experiences to a trusted adult, and always be kind and respectful in online interactions.</p>\n<p><strong>6. What career opportunities does digital technology offer Nigerian youth?</strong> </p>\n<p>Digital technology opens doors to careers such as software development, graphic design, data analysis, cybersecurity, digital marketing, content creation (blogging, YouTube, podcasting), e-commerce, and IT support — many of which can be done from anywhere in Nigeria with just a laptop and internet connection.</p>", "<p><img src=\"assets/img/image12.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\"><strong>INTRODUCTION</strong>:</p>\n<p>Have you ever wondered how the phone or tablet you use to browse the internet came to be? Or how a computer can solve thousands of calculations in less than a second? These amazing machines did not just appear out of nowhere. They are the result of hundreds of years of human thinking, experimenting, failing, and improving.</p>\n<p>From a simple wooden frame with beads to a smartphone that fits in your pocket, the story of digital devices is one of the most fascinating journeys in the history of mankind. For Nigerian students like you, understanding this history is not just for examinations — it helps you appreciate why technology matters, and how you can be part of the next chapter of that story.</p>\n<p><strong><br />\nLEARNING OBJECTIVES</strong></p>\n<p>By the end of this lesson, you should be able to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Identify early calculating tools such as the Abacus and Napier's Bones and explain how they were used</p></li>\n<li><p>Describe the five generations of computers and the key features of each generation</p></li>\n<li><p>Explain the difference between analog and digital devices</p></li>\n<li><p>Trace the evolution of computers from large, room-sized machines to handheld devices</p></li>\n<li><p>Discuss modern digital trends and how they affect everyday life in Nigeria</p></li>\n<li><p>Appreciate the importance of the history of computing in understanding modern technology</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>WHERE IT ALL BEGAN: EARLY CALCULATING TOOLS</h2>\n<p>Long before electricity existed, human beings needed ways to count, record numbers, and solve mathematical problems. Farmers needed to count their crops. Traders needed to calculate profits. Governments needed to track taxes. So people began to create tools that could help them manage numbers more easily.</p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/img/image13.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\"></p>\n<p>These early tools are the ancestors of every computer and digital device we use today.</p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/img/image14.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\"><strong>The Abacus — The World's First Calculator</strong></p>\n<p>The Abacus is widely considered the oldest calculating device in the world. It was invented around 2700 BCE in ancient Mesopotamia (modern-day Iraq) and later developed further in China, where it became very popular.</p>\n<p>The Abacus looks like a rectangular wooden frame with rows of rods or wires. Each rod holds beads that can be slid from one side to another. By moving these beads in specific patterns, a user can perform addition, subtraction, multiplication, and even division.</p>\n<p>Think of the Abacus like a manual calculator — no battery required, no screen, just beads and logic. In fact, a skilled Abacus user can sometimes solve arithmetic problems faster than someone using a basic electronic calculator!    </p>\n<p>In some Asian countries, Abacus use is still taught in schools today as a way to strengthen mental arithmetic. In Nigeria, though we do not use the Abacus in classrooms, some early counting tools made from seeds and stones were used by traders in various communities for similar purposes.</p>\n<p><strong>Napier's Bones — Multiplication Made Easier</strong></p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/img/image15.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\">In 1617, a Scottish mathematician named John Napier invented a clever tool to simplify multiplication and division. He called them \"Napier's Bones\" — not because they were made from actual bones (though some early versions were), but because the rods resembled bones.</p>\n<p>Napier's Bones consisted of a set of numbered rods or strips, each containing multiplication tables. By arranging these rods side by side, a person could read off the answer to complex multiplication problems without having to memorize large tables.</p>\n<p>This was a major breakthrough at the time. Before Napier's Bones, merchants and mathematicians had to do lengthy, error-prone calculations by hand. With this invention, the process became faster and more reliable.</p>\n<p>Napier's Bones were especially useful to traders, engineers, and scientists. Think of it this way — before smartphones, Nigerians in the market used paper and mental math; Napier's tool was like giving those traders a smarter piece of paper.</p>\n<p><strong>Other early tools that followed include:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>The Slide Rule (1620s) — a ruler-like device that used logarithms to perform calculations</p></li>\n<li><p>The Pascaline (1642) — the first mechanical calculator, built by French mathematician Blaise Pascal, which could add and subtract automatically using gears and wheels</p></li>\n<li><p>The Difference Engine (1820s) — designed by Charles Babbage, this steam-powered machine is considered the forerunner of the modern computer</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>THE FIVE GENERATIONS OF COMPUTERS</h2>\n<p>As technology improved, calculating machines became more powerful and more complex. Computer scientists and historians have grouped the development of computers into five major periods called \"generations.\" Each generation is defined by the main technology used to build the machines.</p>\n<p><strong>First Generation Computers (1940s – 1950s): The Age of Vacuum Tubes</strong></p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/img/image16.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\">The first electronic computers appeared in the 1940s. These machines used vacuum tubes — small glass devices that looked like light bulbs — to process information. They also used magnetic drums for memory storage.</p>\n<p>First generation computers were enormous. Some filled entire rooms and weighed several tonnes. They consumed huge amounts of electricity and generated so much heat that air conditioning systems had to run constantly just to keep them from overheating.</p>\n<p><strong>Examples of first-generation computers</strong> include ENIAC (Electronic Numerical Integrator and Computer), which was completed in 1945 in the United States. ENIAC contained over 17,000 vacuum tubes and could perform about 5,000 additions per second — which seemed miraculous at the time.</p>\n<p>Programming these computers was very difficult. Users had to write instructions using machine language (a series of ones and zeros) or physically rewire parts of the machine. These computers were used mainly by governments and large universities for research, military calculations, and census work.</p>\n<p><strong>For Nigerian context</strong>: In the 1940s and early 1950s, Nigeria was still under British colonial rule. While these giant computers existed in the West, Nigerian communities were still using manual record-keeping. The first computers would not reach Nigeria until many years later.</p>\n<p><strong>Second Generation Computers (1950s – 1960s): The Transistor Takes Over</strong></p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/img/image17.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\">In the late 1950s, vacuum tubes were replaced by transistors — small, solid-state devices that could do the same job as a vacuum tube but were much smaller, cheaper, and more reliable. This was a massive improvement.</p>\n<p>Second generation computers were still large, but much smaller than first generation machines. They used less electricity, produced less heat, and were faster. They also introduced magnetic core memory, which was more efficient than magnetic drums.</p>\n<p>Programming also improved during this period. High-level programming languages like FORTRAN (for scientific work) and COBOL (for business tasks) were developed, making it easier to write programs without worrying about machine-level details.</p>\n<p><strong><br />\n</strong></p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/img/image18.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\"><strong>Third Generation Computers (1960s – 1970s): Integrated Circuits Change Everything</strong></p>\n<p>The invention of the integrated circuit (IC) — also called a microchip — in the late 1950s ushered in the third generation of computers. An integrated circuit is a tiny chip of silicon that contains thousands of transistors and electronic components all packed into a space smaller than a fingernail.</p>\n<p>This meant that computers could now be made even smaller, cheaper, and more powerful than before. Third generation computers were the first to be used in multiple industries at once — banking, airlines, hospitals, and even some government offices in developing countries.</p>\n<p>Operating systems were also developed during this era, which allowed computers to run more than one program at a time. This made computers far more practical for everyday use.</p>\n<p><strong>Fourth Generation Computers (1970s – Present): The Microprocessor Era</strong></p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/img/image19.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\">The fourth generation began with the invention of the microprocessor in 1971 by Intel Corporation. A microprocessor is essentially an entire computer's processing unit placed on a single chip.</p>\n<p>This breakthrough made it possible to build small, affordable personal computers — what we know today as PCs (personal computers). Companies like Apple and IBM launched personal computers in the 1970s and 1980s, bringing computing to homes, schools, and small businesses for the first time.</p>\n<p>In Nigeria, fourth generation computers began arriving in significant numbers during the 1980s and 1990s. Nigerian banks like First Bank and UBA adopted computers for record-keeping. Universities began introducing computer laboratories. The National Information Technology Development Agency (NITDA), established in 2001, would later push for wider adoption of computers in schools and government offices.</p>\n<p>Today, our laptops, desktops, tablets, and smartphones all belong to the fourth generation category.</p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/img/image20.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\"><strong>Fifth Generation Computers (Present and Beyond): Artificial Intelligence</strong></p>\n<p>The fifth generation is the current era, though it is still evolving. It is defined by the use of Artificial Intelligence (AI), machine learning, natural language processing, and quantum computing.</p>\n<p>Fifth generation computers are designed to think and learn — not just calculate. They can recognize faces, understand spoken language, suggest music based on your taste, translate languages, and even write text. Examples of fifth generation technology include <img src=\"assets/img/image21.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\">Siri (Apple), Google Assistant, and chatbots used in customer service.</p>\n<p>In Nigeria, fifth generation technology is already affecting daily life. When you use the PiggyVest app to save money, and it sends you spending suggestions, that is AI at work. When you use Google Translate to convert Yoruba or Igbo into English, that is also fifth generation computing.</p>\n<h2>FROM ANALOG TO DIGITAL: UNDERSTANDING THE BIG SHIFT</h2>\n<p>Before we go further, let us understand an important distinction — the difference between analog and digital devices.</p>\n<p><strong>What is an Analog Device?</strong></p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/img/image22.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\">An analog device works with continuous, physical signals. Think of an old wall clock with hands that sweep smoothly around the face — the movement is continuous, not jumping from one position to the next. Old radio sets, vinyl record players, and cassette tape recorders are all analog devices. They store and transmit information in the form of continuous waves.</p>\n<p><strong>What is a Digital Device?</strong></p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/img/image23.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\">A digital device works with discrete values — specifically, information is broken down into binary form using only two values: 0 and 1. Everything you see on a computer screen — text, images, videos, sounds — is ultimately stored as combinations of millions of zeros and ones.</p>\n<p>The shift from analog to digital happened gradually through the latter half of the 20th century. Digital technology offered several advantages:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Better accuracy — digital signals are less affected by noise and interference</p></li>\n<li><p>Easier to store and copy information without quality loss</p></li>\n<li><p>Data can be processed, shared, and secured far more efficiently</p></li>\n<li><p>Devices became smaller as digital components shrank</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p>In Nigeria, this shift is very visible. Older Nigerians remember when NEPA (now AEDC or other distribution companies) used analog meters that could be read by an electrician. Today, prepaid digital meters allow you to top up your electricity the same way you buy airtime. Old analogue television sets have been replaced by digital decoders and smart TVs. Vinyl music has given way to streaming platforms like Boomplay, Audiomack, and Spotify.</p>\n<h2>MODERN DIGITAL TRENDS SHAPING OUR WORLD</h2>\n<p>Technology never stops evolving. Here are some of the major digital trends happening right now — many of which you can already see in Nigerian society:</p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p><strong>Smartphones and Mobile Computing</strong></p></li>\n<li><p>Nigeria has one of the highest smartphone adoption rates in Africa. From Tecno to Infinix to Samsung, millions of Nigerians now use smartphones for banking, schooling, shopping, social media, and entertainment. These pocket-sized computers are more powerful than the giant machines of the 1960s.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Cloud Computing</strong></p></li>\n<li><p>Instead of saving files only on your device, cloud computing allows you to store data on remote servers and access it from anywhere. Google Drive, OneDrive, and iCloud are popular examples. Nigerian businesses increasingly use cloud services to run operations without expensive hardware.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Artificial Intelligence in Everyday Life</strong></p></li>\n<li><p>AI-powered tools are now integrated into banking apps, healthcare services, and even education platforms. WAEC and JAMB are exploring AI-assisted marking and fraud detection systems.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Internet of Things (IoT)</strong></p></li>\n<li><p>This refers to everyday objects connected to the internet — smart TVs, smart meters, smart security cameras. In modern Nigerian homes, IoT devices are gradually becoming common.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Fintech Revolution</strong></p></li>\n<li><p>Companies like Flutterwave, Paystack, Opay, and PalmPay are using digital technology to change how Nigerians send and receive money. These platforms are built on the same computer science principles that began with Napier and Babbage centuries ago.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h2>PRACTICAL APPLICATIONS IN NIGERIA</h2>\n<p>The history of digital devices is not just classroom theory — it has direct practical implications:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Nigerian banks process millions of transactions daily using fourth and fifth generation computers</p></li>\n<li><p>Schools are adopting e-learning platforms like uLesson and Prepclass to teach students digitally</p></li>\n<li><p>Government agencies like INEC use digital devices for voter registration and election management</p></li>\n<li><p>Medical hospitals in Lagos, Abuja, and Port Harcourt use digital scanning equipment and electronic health records</p></li>\n<li><p>Nigerian farmers are beginning to use mobile apps to monitor weather, access market prices, and manage farmland</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>ETHICAL CONSIDERATIONS AND DIGITAL SAFETY</h2>\n<p>As digital devices become more powerful, the responsibility that comes with using them also grows. Here are some important points for every student to keep in mind:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Always protect your personal information online. Never share your passwords, bank details, or home address with strangers on the internet.</p></li>\n<li><p>Respect intellectual property. Do not copy other people's work (software, music, writing) without permission. This is illegal under Nigerian copyright law.</p></li>\n<li><p>Avoid cyberbullying. The same devices that help you learn can also be used to hurt others. Use your devices with respect and kindness.</p></li>\n<li><p>Be mindful of screen time. Spending too many hours on your phone or computer can affect your eyes, sleep, and concentration. Balance is key.</p></li>\n<li><p>Report suspicious activity. If you receive strange messages or feel uncomfortable about something online, report it to a trusted adult.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>CLASSROOM AND HOME ACTIVITIES</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>Activity 1:</strong> Timeline Creation Draw a horizontal timeline on a piece of paper. Mark the major milestones in the history of computing from the Abacus (2700 BCE) to the present day. Label each point with the name of the tool or technology and the year.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Activity 2:</strong> Analog vs Digital Scavenger Hunt Walk around your home or school and list five analog devices and five digital devices you can find. Write a short note beside each one explaining how it works.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Activity 3:</strong> Generation Match Write the five generations of computers in one column and shuffle the following in another column: vacuum tubes, transistors, artificial intelligence, microprocessors, integrated circuits. Draw lines to match each generation with its defining technology.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Activity 4:</strong> Research Project Ask an elder in your family or community about the first computer or digital device they ever used. Write a short paragraph about their experience and compare it to how you use technology today.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>ASSESSMENT QUESTIONS</h2>\n<p><strong>Objective Questions</strong></p>\n<p>1. Which of the following is considered the oldest calculating device?</p>\n<p>A. Napier's Bones</p>\n<p>B. The Abacus</p>\n<p>C. The Pascaline</p>\n<p>D. The Slide Rule</p>\n<p>2. Which component defined the second generation of computers?</p>\n<p>A. Vacuum tubes</p>\n<p>B. Integrated circuits</p>\n<p>C. Transistors</p>\n<p>D. Microprocessors</p>\n<p>3. The device that placed an entire computer processor on a single chip is called a ___</p>\n<p>A. Transistor</p>\n<p>B. Microprocessor</p>\n<p>C. Vacuum tube</p>\n<p>D. RAM chip</p>\n<p>4. What does \"digital\" mean in the context of computing?</p>\n<p>A. Information stored as continuous waves</p>\n<p>B. Information processed using only the values 0 and 1</p>\n<p>C. Information displayed on a screen</p>\n<p>D. Information sent via cables</p>\n<p>5. Which Nigerian fintech company is an example of fifth generation digital technology in action?</p>\n<p>A. NEPA</p>\n<p>B. Flutterwave</p>\n<p>C. NTA</p>\n<p>D. WAEC</p>\n<p><strong>Theory Questions</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p>Explain the difference between analog and digital devices. Give two examples of each from the Nigerian environment.</p></li>\n<li><p>Describe the five generations of computers. For each generation, mention one key technology and one major improvement over the previous generation.</p></li>\n<li><p>What is Napier's Bones and how did it contribute to the development of computing? Why was it considered an improvement at the time it was invented?</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h2>SUMMARY</h2>\n<p>In this lesson, we traced the exciting history of digital devices from the earliest calculating tools to the modern age of Artificial Intelligence. We started with the Abacus — a bead-based counting tool that has been used for thousands of years — and Napier's Bones, a 17th century invention that simplified multiplication. We then explored the five generations of computers, from the massive vacuum tube machines of the 1940s to today's AI-powered devices. We also examined the shift from analog to digital technology and how that transformation shaped everything from Nigerian banking to entertainment. Finally, we discussed modern digital trends — smartphones, cloud computing, IoT, and fintech — that are reshaping Nigerian society right now.</p>\n<h2>CONCLUSION</h2>\n<p>Every time you pick up a smartphone, open a laptop, or use an ATM in Port Harcourt or Kano or Lagos, you are holding the result of thousands of years of human curiosity and invention. The Abacus, Napier's Bones, and the early computers laid the foundation for everything we have today.</p>\n<p>As a JSS 1 student, you are growing up in the most technologically advanced era in human history. Understanding where these tools came from will help you use them more wisely, think more creatively, and maybe — just maybe — inspire you to create the next generation of digital devices that will carry the world forward.</p>\n<p>Nigeria's future depends not just on oil or agriculture, but on young, informed, tech-savvy minds like yours. The journey of computing is still going on — and you are very much a part of it.</p>\n<h2>FREQUENTLY ASKED QUESTIONS (FAQ)</h2>\n<p><strong>1. What is the difference between the Abacus and Napier's Bones?<br />\n</strong>The Abacus is a counting frame using beads on rods and is mainly used for basic arithmetic. Napier's Bones, on the other hand, are a set of numbered rods used specifically to simplify multiplication and division. While both are early calculating tools, Napier's Bones came much later (1617) and solved a more specific mathematical problem.</p>\n<p><strong>2. How many generations of computers are there?<br />\n</strong>There are five recognized generations of computers. Each generation is identified by a major change in the technology used to build and operate the machines — from vacuum tubes in the first generation to artificial intelligence in the fifth.</p>\n<p><strong>3. When did computers first arrive in Nigeria?<br />\n</strong>Computers began arriving in Nigeria in significant numbers during the 1970s and 1980s, mainly in banks, universities, and government institutions. Widespread personal computer use began growing through the 1990s and accelerated significantly with the spread of the internet in the 2000s.</p>\n<p><strong>4. What is the meaning of \"digital\" in digital devices?<br />\n</strong>Digital means that information is represented and processed using binary code — a system of only two values: 0 and 1. Every image, sound, text, or video on your device is ultimately stored as a long string of these zeros and ones.</p>\n<p><strong>5. Is a smartphone a fourth or fifth generation computer?<br />\n</strong>Most smartphones today fall within the fourth-generation category because they are based on microprocessor technology. However, smartphones with built-in AI assistants and machine learning features (like Siri or Google Assistant) incorporate elements of fifth generation computing.</p>\n<p><strong>6. Why is it important to learn about the history of computers?<br />\n</strong>Understanding the history of computers helps you appreciate how technology developed, why things work the way they do, and what challenges scientists had to overcome. It also helps you think critically about where technology might go in the future — and how you can contribute to that journey.</p>", "<p><strong>INTRODUCTION</strong>:</p>\n<p>Have you ever wondered who builds the apps on your phone, fixes your computer when it breaks, or keeps your school's internet running? Behind every digital screen, every WhatsApp message, and every ATM transaction in Nigeria, there are skilled professionals working quietly in the background. These people chose careers in Information Technology — and their work touches millions of lives every single day.</p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/img/image24.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\"></p>\n<p>Information Technology, commonly called IT or ICT, is one of the fastest-growing fields in the world today. In Nigeria alone, the tech industry has been growing at a remarkable pace. Cities like Lagos, Abuja, and Port Harcourt now have tech hubs, innovation centres, and startup companies that are creating thousands of jobs for young Nigerians. Places like the Yaba tech hub in Lagos — sometimes called \"Yabacon Valley\" — are proof that Nigeria is building a serious digital economy.</p>\n<h2>LEARNING OBJECTIVES</h2>\n<p>By the end of this lesson, students should be able to:</p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p>Define Information Technology and explain its role in the Nigerian economy</p></li>\n<li><p>Identify at least five career areas within the ICT industry</p></li>\n<li><p>Describe what professionals in software, hardware, networking, and data roles do</p></li>\n<li><p>Explain emerging digital careers such as Artificial Intelligence, cybersecurity, and robotics</p></li>\n<li><p>List the key skills needed to build a successful career in Information Technology</p></li>\n<li><p>Appreciate the importance of responsible and ethical behaviour in ICT careers</p></li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>WHAT IS INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY AS A CAREER?</strong></p>\n<p>Information Technology as a career simply means making a living by using, building, managing, or protecting digital systems and devices. It is not just one job — it is a whole world of different roles, each with its own responsibilities and rewards.</p>\n<p>Think about this: when you use the MTN or Airtel network to browse the internet, different IT professionals are responsible for making that happen. Someone designed the network, someone else manages the servers, another person protects the system from hackers, and a data analyst studies how people use the network to help the company improve. All of these are ICT careers.</p>\n<p>In Nigeria, the National Information Technology Development Agency (NITDA) has been promoting IT education and careers for years. The government recognises that the future of Nigeria's economy depends heavily on digital skills. This is exactly why your school includes Digital Technology in your timetable.</p>\n<h2>THE MAIN AREAS OF ICT CAREERS</h2>\n<p>Let us look at the major career areas in Information Technology one by one.</p>\n<h2>SOFTWARE CAREERS</h2>\n<p>Software is the set of instructions that tells a computer what to do. Every app, website, and program you use is built by software professionals. Software careers are among the most popular and well-paying in the world today.</p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p><strong>Software Developer or Programmer:</strong> A software developer writes the code that creates programs and applications. For example, the people who built Paystack — a Nigerian online payment platform — are software developers. They wrote the code that allows millions of Nigerians to pay for goods and services online safely.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Web Designer and Web Developer:</strong> A web designer creates the look and layout of websites — choosing colours, fonts, and images. A web developer builds the backend, making sure everything actually works. Many Nigerian businesses today need websites, which means this is a growing career locally.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Mobile App Developer:</strong> These are the professionals who build the apps on your Android or iOS phone. The developers who created OPay, Kuda Bank, or Bolt in Nigeria are mobile app developers. As smartphones become more common across Nigeria, this career is booming.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Game Developer:</strong> Game developers create video games. While Nigeria's gaming industry is still young, it is growing. Studios are beginning to emerge in Lagos and Abuja, and Nigerian game developers are gaining international attention.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h2>HARDWARE CAREERS</h2>\n<p>Hardware refers to the physical parts of a computer — the screen, keyboard, processor, memory chips, and everything you can actually touch. Hardware careers involve building, maintaining, repairing, and upgrading these physical components.</p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p><strong>Computer Hardware Engineer:</strong> A hardware engineer designs and tests the physical components inside computers. These professionals work with manufacturers and technology companies to produce better, faster, and more efficient machines.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Computer Technician:</strong> This is perhaps the most visible hardware career in Nigeria. Computer technicians repair and maintain computers, printers, and other devices. You will find them in shops across Lagos Island, Computer Village in Ikeja, Otigba Street, and in virtually every Nigerian town. This is a career that has provided income for hundreds of thousands of Nigerians.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Electronics and GSM Repair Technician:</strong> Many Nigerians make a living repairing smartphones and electronic gadgets. A GSM repair technician understands the circuits inside phones and can fix them when they break. This skill is highly valued in Nigeria where people often prefer to repair devices rather than replace them.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>IT Support Specialist:</strong> Also called a help desk technician, this professional helps users solve computer and device problems. Banks, government offices, hospitals, and schools all need IT support staff to keep their systems running.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h2>NETWORKING CAREERS</h2>\n<p>A network is what allows computers to communicate with each other — whether within a school building or across the whole world via the internet. Networking professionals build and manage these connections.</p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p><strong>Network Engineer:</strong> A network engineer designs and sets up computer networks. If your school has a computer lab where all the machines are connected, a network engineer was involved in setting that up. Telecom companies like MTN, Glo, Airtel, and 9mobile employ thousands of network engineers in Nigeria.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>System Administrator:</strong> A system administrator (often called a sysadmin) manages and maintains an organisation's computer systems and networks. They make sure everything is running properly, keep software updated, and handle technical problems before they become serious.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Internet Service Provider (ISP) Technician:</strong> These are the professionals who install and maintain internet connections for homes and businesses. Companies like Spectranet, Smile, and ipNX in Nigeria rely on these technicians to serve their customers.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h2>DATA CAREERS</h2>\n<p>Every time you search on Google, buy something online, or stream a video, data is being collected and stored. Data professionals help organisations make sense of all this information.</p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p><strong>Data Analyst:</strong> A data analyst collects and studies data to help organisations make better decisions. For example, a data analyst working for a Nigerian telecommunications company might study call patterns to figure out which network services Nigerians use the most — and then advise the company on how to improve.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Database Administrator (DBA):</strong> A database administrator manages the systems where data is stored — making sure it is secure, organised, and accessible when needed. Banks, hospitals, and government agencies in Nigeria all depend on database administrators to protect important records.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Data Scientist:</strong> This is a more advanced role that uses statistics, mathematics, and programming to find patterns and make predictions from large amounts of data. Data scientists are among the highest-paid professionals in the technology industry globally.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h2>EMERGING DIGITAL CAREERS: THE FUTURE IS NOW</h2>\n<p>Technology never stays still. New career fields are emerging that did not even exist twenty years ago. You might end up working in one of these exciting new areas.</p>\n<h2>ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE (AI) AND MACHINE LEARNING</h2>\n<p>Artificial Intelligence is about building systems that can think and learn — somewhat like a human brain. AI professionals in Nigeria are building smart systems for agriculture (predicting crop yields), healthcare (diagnosing diseases faster), and finance (detecting fraud in banking transactions).</p>\n<p>An AI engineer or Machine Learning specialist designs and trains these intelligent systems. This career requires strong mathematics and programming skills, but it is one of the most exciting and rewarding careers available today.</p>\n<h2>CYBERSECURITY</h2>\n<p>As more Nigerians go online, cyber criminals are also becoming more active. Cybersecurity professionals protect people, businesses, and governments from online attacks. They are sometimes called ethical hackers or security analysts — because they use hacking knowledge to defend systems rather than attack them.</p>\n<p>In Nigeria, cybercrime costs the economy billions of naira each year. The demand for cybersecurity professionals is therefore very high, and this career is expected to grow significantly over the coming decade. The EFCC and other Nigerian agencies also employ cybersecurity experts to track and prosecute online criminals.</p>\n<h2>ROBOTICS AND AUTOMATION</h2>\n<p>Robotics engineers design machines that can perform tasks automatically — from assembling cars in factories to delivering packages. While this field is still developing in Nigeria, robotics competitions for secondary school students are now being held in Nigerian cities, giving young people like you an early introduction to this exciting career.</p>\n<h2>CLOUD COMPUTING</h2>\n<p>Cloud computing professionals manage internet-based systems that store and process data remotely. Rather than storing everything on one local computer, cloud systems allow information to be accessed from anywhere. Companies like Amazon (AWS), Microsoft (Azure), and Google offer cloud services, and Nigerian professionals trained in cloud computing are increasingly in demand.</p>\n<h2>DIGITAL MARKETING AND CONTENT CREATION</h2>\n<p>Not all tech careers require heavy coding. Digital marketers help businesses reach their customers online using social media, email, search engines, and websites. Content creators produce videos, podcasts, blog posts, and graphics that attract and inform online audiences. In Nigeria, many young people have built businesses and careers through YouTube, Instagram, and TikTok.</p>\n<p>UI/UX Design UI stands for User Interface — how a website or app looks. UX stands for User Experience — how easy it is to use. UI/UX designers focus on making digital products beautiful and easy to navigate. A good UI/UX designer is worth their weight in gold to any tech company.</p>\n<h2>SKILLS NEEDED FOR A SUCCESSFUL ICT CAREER</h2>\n<p>Knowing that these careers exist is one thing. Preparing for them is another. The good news is that you can begin building the right skills right now.</p>\n<p><strong>Technical Skills</strong></p>\n<p>These are the practical abilities that are directly related to technology work:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Basic computer operation and typing</p></li>\n<li><p>Understanding of how software and hardware work together</p></li>\n<li><p>Programming knowledge (you can start with Scratch, Python, or HTML)</p></li>\n<li><p>Understanding of networks and internet systems</p></li>\n<li><p>Data analysis using tools like spreadsheets</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Soft Skills</strong></p>\n<p>Many people underestimate how important these non-technical skills are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Problem-solving: IT professionals encounter new challenges every day</p></li>\n<li><p>Critical thinking: the ability to analyse a situation before jumping to solutions</p></li>\n<li><p>Communication: explaining technical ideas to non-technical people</p></li>\n<li><p>Teamwork: most IT projects involve working in groups</p></li>\n<li><p>Adaptability: technology changes quickly, and professionals must keep learning</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Digital Literacy</strong></p>\n<p>Understanding how to use digital tools responsibly, safely, and effectively is the foundation of any ICT career. This is exactly why Digital Technology is part of your school curriculum.</p>\n<h2>PRACTICAL APPLICATIONS IN NIGERIA</h2>\n<p>Let us bring all of this closer to home.</p>\n<p>When you visit a Nigerian bank to deposit money, an IT support specialist maintains the machines, a network engineer keeps the bank's systems connected, a database administrator protects your account information, and a cybersecurity analyst watches for suspicious transactions — all happening at the same time.</p>\n<p>When your parents use POS machines to pay at a shop in Port Harcourt or Kano, mobile developers, payment system engineers, and network professionals made that possible.</p>\n<p>When a farmer in Kogi State receives weather updates on his mobile phone to decide when to plant, data scientists and AI engineers made that information available.</p>\n<p>Every day in Nigeria, ICT professionals are quietly making life easier, safer, and more connected.</p>\n<h2>ADVANTAGES OF A CAREER IN ICT</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p>High earning potential, especially in software development, cybersecurity, and data science</p></li>\n<li><p>Remote work opportunities — many IT jobs can be done from home or from anywhere in Nigeria</p></li>\n<li><p>High demand globally — Nigerian tech professionals are working for international companies</p></li>\n<li><p>Variety of roles — there is something for every interest and talent</p></li>\n<li><p>Constant growth and learning — technology never stops evolving, which makes it an exciting lifelong career</p></li>\n<li><p>Opportunity to build your own startup — many successful Nigerian tech companies were started by young Nigerians with coding skills</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>CHALLENGES AND THINGS TO WATCH OUT FOR</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p>The tech industry changes fast — you must commit to continuous learning</p></li>\n<li><p>Some roles require years of study and practice before you become proficient</p></li>\n<li><p>Power supply and internet access in Nigeria can be obstacles, especially outside major cities</p></li>\n<li><p>Cybercrime and unethical hacking are serious legal risks — IT knowledge must always be used responsibly</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>ETHICAL AND SAFETY CONSIDERATIONS</h2>\n<p>Every ICT professional carries a responsibility to use technology ethically. This means:</p>\n<p>Never using your skills to harm others, steal data, or invade people's privacy. In Nigeria, the Cybercrime (Prohibition, Prevention, etc.) Act of 2015 makes it a criminal offence to hack systems, steal data, or conduct online fraud. Young people who misuse technology face serious legal consequences.</p>\n<p>Respecting intellectual property means not stealing software, music, films, or written work that belongs to others. Giving credit to the creators and developers whose work you use is both ethical and professional.</p>\n<p>Protecting users' data is a sacred responsibility. Whether you become a developer, DBA, or network engineer, the information people trust you with must be handled with care and honesty.</p>\n<h2>CLASSROOM AND HOME ACTIVITIES</h2>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p><strong>Activity 1</strong> — Career Matching Game Write the names of five ICT career areas on a sheet of paper (Software, Hardware, Networking, Data, Cybersecurity). Next to each, write one Nigerian company or industry where that career is needed. Share with your classmates and discuss.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Activity 2</strong> — Technology Around Me Walk around your home or school and list ten things that require IT professionals to work. For each item, try to name the type of IT career involved. (Example: the school printer — IT Support Technician.)</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Activity 3</strong> — My Future IT Career Write a short paragraph (five to ten sentences) describing the ICT career you would most like to pursue. Explain what you find interesting about it and how it could help Nigeria.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Activity 4</strong> — Research Challenge Ask an adult in your family or community who works with computers or phones to describe what they do for a living. Write down what you learn and present it to your class.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h2>ASSESSMENT QUESTIONS</h2>\n<p><strong>Objective Questions</strong></p>\n<p>1. Which of the following is an example of a software career?</p>\n<p>A. Computer Technician</p>\n<p>B. Web Developer</p>\n<p>C. Network Cable Installer</p>\n<p>D. Printer Repairman</p>\n<p>2. The professional who protects computer systems from online attacks is called a:</p>\n<p>A. Data Analyst</p>\n<p>B. Web Designer</p>\n<p>C. Cybersecurity Specialist</p>\n<p>D. Hardware Engineer</p>\n<p>3. Which Nigerian city is known for having a major technology hub sometimes called \"Yabacon Valley\"?</p>\n<p>A. Kano</p>\n<p>B. Port Harcourt</p>\n<p>C. Lagos</p>\n<p>D. Enugu</p>\n<p>4. A database administrator is mainly responsible for:</p>\n<p>A. Writing mobile apps</p>\n<p>B. Teaching students how to type</p>\n<p>C. Managing and protecting stored data</p>\n<p>D. Installing internet cables</p>\n<p>5. Which of the following is NOT a soft skill needed for ICT careers?</p>\n<p>A. Teamwork</p>\n<p>B. Communication</p>\n<p>C. Soldering iron usage</p>\n<p>D. Problem-solving</p>\n<p><strong>Theory Questions</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p>Explain the difference between a software career and a hardware career in Information Technology. Give one example of each.</p></li>\n<li><p>Describe three emerging digital careers and explain why each one is important for Nigeria's future development.</p></li>\n<li><p>Why is it important for ICT professionals in Nigeria to behave ethically? Mention at least two laws or rules that guide responsible use of technology in Nigeria.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h2>SUMMARY</h2>\n<p>In this lesson, we explored the wide world of career opportunities in Information Technology. We learned that ICT careers are grouped into areas including software development, hardware engineering, networking, data management, and emerging fields like Artificial Intelligence, cybersecurity, robotics, and cloud computing. We saw how these careers are already shaping Nigerian society — from banking and agriculture to education and entertainment. We also discussed the technical and soft skills needed to succeed, and the ethical responsibilities that come with working in technology.</p>\n<h2>CONCLUSION</h2>\n<p>The world is changing fast, and technology is driving that change. Nigeria is not left behind — in fact, the country is home to some of Africa's most exciting tech innovations. As a JSS 1 student, you are at the perfect age to begin thinking seriously about where you fit in this digital future.</p>\n<p>You do not need to wait until university to start preparing. Learning to type properly, understanding how computers work, exploring basic programming, and developing problem-solving habits are all steps you can take right now. The careers we discussed in this lesson are not distant dreams — they are real possibilities waiting for students who are willing to learn, practise, and grow.</p>\n<p>Nigeria needs its own software developers, cybersecurity experts, AI engineers, and data scientists. Perhaps one of them is you.</p>\n<h2>FREQUENTLY ASKED QUESTIONS</h2>\n<p><strong>1. What is the best ICT career to study in Nigeria?<br />\n</strong>There is no single \"best\" career — it depends on your interests and strengths. However, software development, cybersecurity, and data science are currently among the highest-demand and best-paying ICT careers both in Nigeria and globally. Choose the area that genuinely excites you.</p>\n<p><strong>2. Do I need to be good at Mathematics to work in IT?<br />\n</strong>For many ICT careers — especially programming, data science, and AI — a solid foundation in mathematics is very helpful. However, careers like UI/UX design, digital marketing, and IT support rely more on creativity, communication, and logical thinking than advanced maths.</p>\n<p><strong>3. Can I learn ICT skills without going to university in Nigeria?<br />\n</strong>Yes. Many successful Nigerian IT professionals are self-taught or trained through vocational programmes, online platforms, coding bootcamps, and professional certifications. While a university degree is valuable, practical skills and experience often matter more in the tech industry.</p>\n<p><strong>4. What companies in Nigeria hire ICT professionals?<br />\n</strong>Many organisations across Nigeria hire IT professionals, including banks (Access Bank, GTBank, Zenith Bank), telecom companies (MTN, Airtel, Glo), technology startups (Flutterwave, Paystack, Andela), government agencies (NITDA, EFCC, NCC), hospitals, schools, and media houses.</p>\n<p><strong>5. Is cybersecurity a good career for young Nigerians?<br />\n</strong>Absolutely. Cybersecurity is one of the fastest-growing career fields in the world, and Nigeria's increasing use of digital banking, e-commerce, and government services means the demand for cybersecurity professionals locally is rising rapidly. It is a career that combines problem-solving, technology, and national security.</p>\n<p><strong>6. At what age should I start preparing for an ICT career?<br />\n</strong>You can start right now — regardless of your age. Simple steps like learning how to type, practising basic computer operations, exploring free coding tutorials online, and paying close attention in your Digital Technology classes are excellent ways to begin your journey towards a rewarding ICT career.</p>", "<p><img src=\"assets/img/image25.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\"><strong>INTRODUCTION</strong></p>\n<p>Walk into any bank, school, hospital, or government office in Nigeria today, and you will find computers sitting on desks, doing work that used to take people hours to finish. From the JAMB CBT centres in Abuja and Lagos to the cybercafés in Aba and Kaduna, computers have become part of everyday Nigerian life. But have you ever stopped to wonder — what exactly makes up a computer? What are those parts you see people touching, clicking, and staring at?</p>\n<p>That is exactly what this lesson is about. Before you can learn how to use a computer properly, you need to understand what it is made of. Just like a car has an engine, tyres, and a steering wheel — all working together — a computer also has different parts, and each one has a job to do.</p>\n<h2>LEARNING OBJECTIVES</h2>\n<p>By the end of this lesson, students should be able to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Define the term \"computer hardware\" in their own words</p></li>\n<li><p>Identify common input and output devices used in computer systems</p></li>\n<li><p>Explain the functions of at least five hardware components</p></li>\n<li><p>Distinguish between input devices and output devices</p></li>\n<li><p>Describe simple and practical maintenance habits for computer hardware</p></li>\n<li><p>Demonstrate responsible care and handling of computer equipment</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>WHAT IS COMPUTER HARDWARE?</strong></p>\n<p>Let us start from the beginning. The word \"hardware\" simply refers to the physical parts of a computer — the parts you can actually touch and feel. If you can pick it up, plug it in, or press it, it is most likely hardware.</p>\n<p>Computer hardware is different from software. Software is the set of programmes and instructions that tell the computer what to do — things like Microsoft Word, Google Chrome, or a game you play. You cannot hold software in your hands. But hardware? You can see it, carry it, and — unfortunately — break it if you are not careful.</p>\n<p>Hardware components work together as a system. None of them is very useful on its own. The keyboard needs the computer to process what you type, and the monitor needs the computer to send it something to display. Everything depends on everything else.</p>\n<h2>CATEGORIES OF COMPUTER HARDWARE</h2>\n<p>Computer hardware is generally grouped into three broad categories:</p>\n<p><strong>Input Devices</strong> — These send information into the computer. </p>\n<p><strong>Output Devices</strong> — These bring information out of the computer for the user to see, hear, or use. </p>\n<p><strong>Processing and Storage Devices</strong> — These include the CPU (the brain of the computer) and memory units that store and process data. (We will cover these in more detail in later topics.)</p>\n<p>For this lesson, our main focus is on input and output devices.</p>\n<p><strong>INPUT DEVICES — SENDING INFORMATION INTO THE COMPUTER</strong></p>\n<p>An input device is any hardware component that allows a user to enter data or instructions into a computer system. In simple terms, input devices are how you \"talk\" to the computer.</p>\n<p>Here are the most common input devices you should know:</p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/img/image26.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\"><strong>The Keyboard</strong></p>\n<p>The keyboard is probably the most popular input device in the world. It looks a lot like a typewriter and has rows of keys with letters, numbers, and special symbols. When you press a key, a signal is sent to the computer, which then displays the corresponding character on the screen.</p>\n<p>In Nigeria, you will find keyboards in computer labs, offices, internet cafés, and even attached to some smart TVs. Standard keyboards have about 104 keys, but laptop keyboards are slightly smaller.</p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/img/image27.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\"><strong>The Mouse</strong></p>\n<p>The mouse is a small handheld device that you move across a flat surface to control a pointer (cursor) on the screen. It usually has two buttons (left and right) and a scroll wheel in the middle. You use it to click on icons, open files, and navigate menus.</p>\n<p>The name \"mouse\" came from early researchers who thought its shape and attached wire looked like a tiny mouse with a tail!</p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/img/image28.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\"><strong>The Scanner</strong></p>\n<p>A scanner is used to copy physical documents or images and convert them into digital files that the computer can store or display. For example, if a school in Rivers State wants to keep digital records of students' birth certificates, they can use a scanner to create a digital copy of each document.</p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/img/image29.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\"><br />\n<strong>The Microphone</strong></p>\n<p>A microphone allows users to input sound into the computer. It captures your voice and converts it into digital data. Today, microphones are built into most laptops and smartphones. They are used for voice calls, recording audio, and voice search.</p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/img/image30.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\"><strong>The Webcam</strong></p>\n<p>A webcam is a small camera connected to the computer that captures live video. During the COVID-19 period in Nigeria, many students and teachers used webcams for online classes via platforms like Zoom and Google Meet. Webcams are input devices because they send visual data into the computer.</p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/img/image31.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\"><strong>The Joystick</strong></p>\n<p>A joystick is mainly used for playing computer games. It has a stick-like handle that can be moved in different directions to control movement on the screen. Many gaming lounges in Nigerian cities use joysticks alongside gaming consoles.</p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/img/image32.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\"><strong>The Touchscreen</strong></p>\n<p>Many modern devices — phones, tablets, ATMs, and even some laptops — have touchscreens. A touchscreen acts as both an input and output device. When you tap or swipe on it, you are giving input to the computer.</p>\n<p><strong>OUTPUT DEVICES — WHAT THE COMPUTER GIVES BACK TO YOU</strong></p>\n<p>After the computer receives your input and processes it, it needs a way to communicate the result back to you. That is where output devices come in. An output device is any hardware that presents processed information from the computer to the user.</p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/img/image33.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\"><strong>The Monitor (Screen)</strong></p>\n<p>The monitor is the most common output device. It looks like a television screen and displays text, images, videos, and everything else the computer wants to show you. Modern monitors are flat and slim (called LCD or LED monitors), unlike the older, bulky ones (CRT monitors) that were common in Nigerian schools and offices in the early 2000s.</p>\n<p><strong>The Printer</strong></p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/img/image34.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\">A printer produces a hard copy — a physical, printed version — of documents and images from the computer. If you type an assignment in Microsoft Word and want to submit it on paper, you send it to the printer. In Nigeria, you will find printers in business centres, schools, banks, and offices everywhere.</p>\n<p>There are different types of printers:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Inkjet printers (common in homes and small offices)</p></li>\n<li><p>Laser printers (faster, used in big offices)</p></li>\n<li><p>Dot matrix printers (older type, still used in some banks for printing receipts)</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p><img src=\"assets/img/image35.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\"><strong>The Speaker</strong></p>\n<p>Speakers output sound from the computer. Whether you are watching a Nollywood film, listening to music, or attending an online class, the speakers are what allow you to hear the audio. Some computers have built-in speakers, while others use external ones connected via cable or Bluetooth.</p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/img/image36.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\"><strong>The Projector</strong></p>\n<p>A projector is used to display a large image of the computer screen onto a wall or white board. Teachers use projectors to show lessons to an entire class, and business people use them for presentations. Many Nigerian secondary schools now use projectors in their ICT labs and classrooms.</p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/img/image37.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\"><strong>The Headphones / Earphones</strong></p>\n<p>Headphones and earphones are personal audio output devices. Instead of broadcasting sound to the whole room, they deliver it directly to one listener. Students use them in computer labs, libraries, and language labs.</p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/img/image38.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\"><strong>The Plotter</strong></p>\n<p>A plotter is a special type of output device used to print large drawings, designs, and architectural plans. Engineers and architects use plotters to produce precise technical drawings. You may find them in engineering firms or printing companies in cities like Lagos and Port Harcourt.</p>\n<h2>PRACTICAL APPLICATIONS IN EVERYDAY NIGERIAN LIFE</h2>\n<p>Understanding hardware is not just for passing exams. Here is how these devices show up in real life around us:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>At the bank, you use a keyboard and touchscreen to operate the ATM (a special computer). The ATM screen (monitor) is an output device that shows your balance, and the receipt printer gives you a printed output.</p></li>\n<li><p>During WAEC and NECO CBT exams, students sit in front of computers with monitors, keyboards, and mice. Knowing how these work gives you confidence.</p></li>\n<li><p>Market traders in Onitsha and Lagos who use POS (Point of Sale) machines are interacting with a small computer that has a touchscreen input and a receipt printer for output.</p></li>\n<li><p>NYSC orientation camp registrations, NIN enrolment, and BVN capture all use biometric scanners (input devices) and computers with screens (output devices).</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>SIMPLE MAINTENANCE PRACTICES FOR COMPUTER HARDWARE</h2>\n<p>One of the best things you can do as a young ICT student is learn how to take care of computer equipment. Computer hardware is expensive, and poor maintenance can damage it quickly. Here are some simple but important habits:</p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p><strong>Keep computers clean.</strong> Dust is a silent enemy of computers. It blocks ventilation holes and causes the machine to overheat. Use a soft, dry cloth to clean the monitor and keyboard regularly. Avoid using water or wet cloths directly on any hardware.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Handle hardware with care.</strong> Never slam the keyboard, drop the mouse, or press the monitor screen with your fingers unnecessarily. These actions can cause damage over time.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Keep food and drinks away.</strong> Liquids spilled on a keyboard or laptop can cause a short circuit and permanently damage the device. Many computer labs in Nigeria have a strict \"no food or drink\" rule for this reason.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Shut down properly.</strong> Always shut down the computer using the proper procedure — through the Start menu or power button — rather than just pulling the plug. Sudden power cuts can corrupt files and damage the hard drive.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Use a surge protector.</strong> Nigeria's power supply situation means voltage fluctuations are common. A surge protector or UPS (Uninterruptible Power Supply) protects your computer from sudden power surges that can burn internal components.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Keep the environment cool and dry.</strong> Computers work best in cool, dry environments. Avoid placing them near windows where rain can enter, or in very hot, stuffy rooms without ventilation.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Report damaged cables immediately.</strong> Frayed or broken cables are dangerous. If you notice a damaged cable in your school lab, report it to your teacher right away. Never use equipment with exposed wires.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h2>SAFETY AND ETHICAL CONSIDERATIONS</h2>\n<p>As a student using computer equipment, you have certain responsibilities:</p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p>Do not tamper with hardware components. Opening the computer casing or removing parts without permission can damage the equipment and may be considered sabotage.</p></li>\n<li><p>Respect shared equipment. Computers in school labs belong to everyone. Treat them as carefully as you would want someone else to treat your own things.</p></li>\n<li><p>Avoid downloading or connecting unknown devices. Plugging in a random flash drive from an unknown source can introduce viruses that damage the computer's software — and eventually, its hardware performance too.</p></li>\n<li><p>Report technical issues rather than trying to fix them yourself. If something goes wrong in the lab, tell your teacher. Attempting to repair hardware without training can make things worse.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h2>CLASSROOM AND HOME ACTIVITIES</h2>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p><strong>Activity 1 — Hardware Hunt</strong> Look around your home or classroom and write down five objects you think could be computer hardware. For each one, write whether it is an input device, an output device, or neither.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Activity 2 — Label the Diagram</strong> Draw a simple computer system (monitor, keyboard, mouse, CPU box, and speakers). Label each part and state whether it is an input or output device.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Activity 3 — Maintenance Role Play</strong> In groups, act out a scenario where a student spills water on a keyboard. Discuss: What happened? What should have been done to prevent it? What should be done now?</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Activity 4 — Device Sorting Game</strong> Write the names of ten hardware devices on separate pieces of paper. Then sort them into two groups: INPUT and OUTPUT. Compare your groupings with a classmate and discuss any differences.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h2>ASSESSMENT QUESTIONS</h2>\n<p><strong>Section A — Objective Questions</strong></p>\n<p>1. Which of the following is an input device?</p>\n<p>A. Monitor</p>\n<p>B. Speaker</p>\n<p>C. Keyboard </p>\n<p>D. Printer</p>\n<p>2. The part of the computer you can physically touch is called:</p>\n<p>A. Software</p>\n<p>B. Hardware </p>\n<p>C. Program</p>\n<p>D. Application</p>\n<p>3. Which output device produces a hard copy of a document?</p>\n<p>A. Scanner</p>\n<p>B. Webcam</p>\n<p>C. Microphone</p>\n<p>D. Printer </p>\n<p>4. What is the main function of a monitor?</p>\n<p>A. To store data</p>\n<p>B. To input sound</p>\n<p>C. To display information </p>\n<p>D. To process instructions</p>\n<p>5. Which of the following is a good computer maintenance habit?</p>\n<p>A. Placing food near the keyboard</p>\n<p>B. Pulling the power plug to shut down</p>\n<p>C. Using a surge protector </p>\n<p>D. Pressing the screen with sharp objects</p>\n<p><strong>Section B — Theory Questions</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p>Define computer hardware and give three examples of hardware components you have learned about in this lesson.</p></li>\n<li><p>Explain the difference between an input device and an output device. Give two examples of each.</p></li>\n<li><p>Describe four simple maintenance practices that can help keep a computer in good working condition. Explain why each practice is important.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h2>SUMMARY</h2>\n<p>In this lesson, we covered quite a lot of ground. Here is a quick recap of the key points:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Computer hardware refers to the physical, tangible parts of a computer system.</p></li>\n<li><p>Input devices allow users to send data into the computer. Examples include the keyboard, mouse, scanner, microphone, webcam, and joystick.</p></li>\n<li><p>Output devices present processed information to the user. Examples include the monitor, printer, speaker, projector, and headphones.</p></li>\n<li><p>Good maintenance practices — such as keeping computers clean, shutting down properly, using surge protectors, and keeping food and drinks away — help to extend the life of computer hardware.</p></li>\n<li><p>Responsible and ethical use of computer equipment is important for every student.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>CONCLUSION</h2>\n<p>Every big journey starts with small steps, and for any student who wants to understand computers, learning about hardware is the very first step. The devices we discussed in this lesson — keyboards, monitors, printers, scanners, and the rest — are the building blocks of every computer system you will ever use, from the school lab to the corporate office.</p>\n<h2>FREQUENTLY ASKED QUESTIONS (FAQ)</h2>\n<p><strong>1. What is the difference between hardware and software?</strong> </p>\n<p>Hardware refers to the physical parts of a computer that you can touch — like the keyboard, monitor, and printer. Software refers to the programmes and instructions that run on the computer, like Microsoft Word or Google Chrome. You can see and touch hardware, but you cannot touch software.</p>\n<p><strong>2. Is a touchscreen an input or output device?</strong> <br />\nA touchscreen is actually both. It functions as an output device by displaying images and information, and as an input device because it allows you to interact with the computer by tapping and swiping on the surface.</p>\n<p><strong>3. Why is it important to shut down a computer properly?</strong> <br />\nShutting down a computer properly ensures that all running programmes close correctly, files are saved, and the operating system finishes its processes safely. Sudden shutdowns — like pulling the power plug — can corrupt files and gradually damage the hard drive.</p>\n<p><strong>4. What should I do if I accidentally spill water on a keyboard?</strong> <br />\nFirst, do not panic. Turn off the computer or disconnect the keyboard immediately. Turn the keyboard upside down to drain the water and let it dry completely — for at least 24–48 hours — before using it again. If it belongs to your school, report the incident to your teacher immediately.</p>\n<p><strong>5. Can a printer be both an input and an output device?</strong> <br />\nStandard printers are output devices because they produce printed copies of documents. However, a multifunction printer (also called an all-in-one printer) that includes a scanner can also act as an input device when it scans documents into the computer.</p>\n<p><strong>6. How often should computer hardware be cleaned?</strong> <br />\nIdeally, computer equipment should be dusted and cleaned at least once a week in busy environments like school labs, and at least once or twice a month in home settings. Regular cleaning prevents dust buildup, which can cause overheating and hardware failure.</p>", "<p><strong>INTRODUCTION</strong>:</p>\n<p>Storage is one of the most important aspects of how a computer works. It is what separates a computer from a simple calculator. Without storage, a computer cannot save your work, run programs, or even start up in the morning. Whether you are watching a video on your phone, submitting a JAMB form online, or saving a PowerPoint presentation for your school assignment, storage is always at work behind the scenes.</p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/img/image39.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\"></p>\n<p>In this lesson, we are going to break down everything you need to know about computer storage units — what they are, how they work, the different types, and how we measure them. By the time you finish reading, topics like RAM, ROM, hard disk, flash drive, GB, and TB will no longer sound like foreign language to you.</p>\n<h2>LEARNING OBJECTIVES:</h2>\n<p><strong>By the end of this lesson, JSS 1 students should be able to:</strong></p>\n<p>1. Define the term \"storage unit\" and explain its role in a computer system</p>\n<p>2. Distinguish between primary storage and secondary storage</p>\n<p>3. Identify examples of primary and secondary storage devices (RAM, ROM, hard disk, flash drive)</p>\n<p>4. Describe the functions of RAM and ROM in a computer</p>\n<p>5. Explain storage measurements from bit to terabyte with correct relationships</p>\n<p>6. Demonstrate understanding of storage units through practical, real-life Nigerian examples</p>\n<h2>STORAGE UNIT:</h2>\n<p>A<img src=\"assets/img/image40.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\"> storage unit in a computer is any hardware component that is used to store data, information, or instructions — either <strong>temporarily</strong> or <strong>permanently</strong>.</p>\n<p>Think of storage the same way you think of your school bag. Your bag holds your textbooks, notebooks, pens, and other items you need throughout the day. Similarly, a computer's storage unit holds programs, files, photos, videos, and operating system instructions that the computer needs to function.</p>\n<p>There are two main categories of storage in a computer:</p>\n<p>- Primary Storage (also called Main Memory or Internal Memory)</p>\n<p>- Secondary Storage (also called External Memory or Auxiliary Memory)</p>\n<p><em><strong>Let us look at each one in detail.</strong></em></p>\n<p><strong>PRIMARY STORAGE — WHAT HAPPENS INSIDE THE COMPUTER</strong></p>\n<h2>PRIMARY STORAGE (MAIN MEMORY)</h2>\n<p>Primary storage refers to the memory that the computer's processor can directly access. It is the working memory of the computer — the place where data and instructions are held while the computer is actively using them.</p>\n<p>Primary storage is fast, but it is usually small in size compared to secondary storage. There are two main types:</p>\n<p><strong>1. RAM — Random Access Memory</strong></p>\n<p><strong>2. ROM — Read-Only Memory</strong></p>\n<p>These two are completely different from each other, and it is important you understand why.</p>\n<p><strong>RAM — RANDOM ACCESS MEMORY</strong></p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/img/image41.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\"><strong>RAM</strong> is the temporary working space of your computer. When you open an application — say, Microsoft Word or a browser to check your school portal — the computer loads that program into <strong>RAM</strong> so it can run quickly.</p>\n<p>The key word here is <strong>temporary</strong>. RAM only holds data while the computer is switched on. Once you turn the computer off, everything in RAM disappears. This is exactly why you lost your work in our opening story — it was stored in RAM, and when the power went off, it vanished.</p>\n<p>Real-Life Nigerian Example: Imagine you are grinding pepper in a blender to make stew. The pepper inside the blender while the machine is running represents data in RAM. The moment you switch off the blender, the pepper is gone from the blades — it has to go somewhere else (a bowl or pot) if you want to keep it.</p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/img/image42.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\"><br />\n<strong>Key Features of RAM:</strong></p>\n<p>- Volatile memory (loses data when power is off)</p>\n<p>- Fast access speed</p>\n<p>- Directly accessed by the CPU (Central Processing Unit)</p>\n<p>- Temporary storage</p>\n<p>- The more RAM a computer has, the faster and smoother it runs</p>\n<p>Common RAM sizes you will see today: 4GB, 8GB, 16GB</p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/img/image43.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\"><strong>ROM — READ-ONLY MEMORY</strong></p>\n<p><strong>ROM</strong> is very different from RAM. As the name suggests, the data stored in <strong>ROM</strong> can only be read — it cannot be changed or erased by the user under normal conditions. More importantly, <strong>ROM</strong> is non-volatile, which means it keeps its data even when the computer is switched off.</p>\n<p>What is stored in <strong>ROM</strong>? ROM contains the instructions that tell the computer how to start up. When you press the power button on a computer, the very first thing that runs is a program stored in ROM called the BIOS (Basic Input/Output System). This program checks the computer's hardware and begins the startup process.</p>\n<p>Real-Life Nigerian Example: Think of ROM like the preset recipe a Mama Put (local canteen) owner has memorised after years of cooking. She does not need to look it up — it is permanently fixed in her memory. Similarly, ROM permanently holds the computer's startup instructions, and they do not change even when the power goes off.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Features of ROM:</strong></p>\n<p>- Non-volatile memory (retains data without power)</p>\n<p>- Data is written once by the manufacturer</p>\n<p>- Cannot be modified by regular users</p>\n<p>- Contains the computer's startup instructions (BIOS/Firmware)</p>\n<p>- Small in size but extremely important</p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/img/image44.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\"><br />\n<strong>COMPARING RAM AND ROM</strong></p>\n<p>Here is an easy way to remember the difference:</p>\n<p><strong>RAM</strong> — Temporary, loses data without power, used for running programs</p>\n<p><strong>ROM</strong> — Permanent, keeps data without power, used for startup instructions</p>\n<p><strong>SECONDARY STORAGE — SAVING YOUR WORK FOR LATER</strong></p>\n<h2>SECONDARY STORAGE</h2>\n<p>Secondary storage is where data is saved permanently (or semi-permanently). Unlike RAM, data stored here remains even after you switch off the computer. This is where your documents, music, videos, and software are kept.</p>\n<p>Secondary storage is generally slower than RAM but can hold much larger amounts of data. There are several types, and you have probably used some of them already.</p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/img/image45.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\"><strong>HARD DISK DRIVE (HDD)</strong></p>\n<p>The hard disk drive, commonly called the hard disk or HDD, is the main storage device found inside most desktop and laptop computers. It stores the operating system (like Windows), all installed software, and all user files.</p>\n<p>Hard disks work by storing data on spinning magnetic platters. Because of this, they can store very large amounts of data at relatively low cost — but they are slower than newer storage technologies.</p>\n<p>Modern hard disks can store anywhere from 500GB to several terabytes (TB) of data.</p>\n<p>Real-Life Nigerian Example: If RAM is the pepper in the blender, the hard disk is your kitchen freezer. You store large quantities of food there for long periods. Even when you switch off the freezer, the food remains (until you take it out). Similarly, your computer files stay on the hard disk even after shutdown.</p>\n<h2>SOLID STATE DRIVE (SSD)</h2>\n<p><img src=\"assets/img/image46.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\">An <strong>SSD</strong> works like a hard disk but uses flash memory instead of spinning platters. This makes <strong>SSDs</strong> much faster, quieter, and more durable — but they are also more expensive.</p>\n<p>Many newer laptops, especially high-end ones, now come with SSDs. If you have ever noticed that some computers start up in less than 10 seconds, they most likely have an SSD.</p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/img/image47.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\"></p>\n<h2>FLASH DRIVE (USB DRIVE / MEMORY STICK)</h2>\n<p><img src=\"assets/img/image48.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\">The flash drive — also called a USB drive, pen drive, or memory stick — is one of the most popular storage devices in Nigeria today. It is small, portable, and does not require any external power.</p>\n<p>You plug it into a USB port on your computer to transfer or store files. Students in Nigeria regularly use flash drives to carry assignments, e-books, past question papers, and presentations from one place to another.</p>\n<p><strong>Common flash drive sizes:</strong> 4GB, 8GB, 16GB, 32GB, 64GB, 128GB</p>\n<p>Flash drives use the same kind of flash memory technology as SSDs, which means they are non-volatile — your data stays safe even when you remove the drive.</p>\n<p>Real-Life Nigerian Example: Think of a flash drive like a small takeaway pack from a restaurant. You fill it up, carry it wherever you are going, and access the content when you need it. That is exactly how a flash drive works — portable storage you can carry in your pocket.</p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/img/image49.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\"><strong>MEMORY CARD (SD CARD)</strong></p>\n<p>Memory cards — often called SD cards (Secure Digital cards) — are small, thin storage devices commonly used in phones, digital cameras, and tablets. Many Nigerian students have memory cards in their Android phones for storing music, videos, and offline content.</p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/img/image50.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\"><strong>Common sizes: 16GB, 32GB, 64GB, 128GB, 256GB</strong></p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/img/image51.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\"><strong>OPTICAL DISC (CD, DVD, BLU-RAY)</strong></p>\n<p>Optical discs store data using laser technology. They were very popular in Nigeria in the early 2000s for music, Nollywood movies, and software installation. CDs can hold about 700MB of data, DVDs up to 4.7GB, and Blu-ray discs up to 50GB or more.</p>\n<p>While optical discs are less common today due to the rise of USB drives and internet downloads, they are still used in some environments and are part of the NERDC curriculum.</p>\n<h2>CLOUD STORAGE</h2>\n<p><img src=\"assets/img/image52.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\">Cloud storage is a modern form of secondary storage where your data is saved on remote servers accessed through the internet. Services like Google Drive, Dropbox, and iCloud are examples. Nigerian students and businesses increasingly use cloud storage to back up important files.</p>\n<p>With cloud storage, you can access your files from any device, anywhere — as long as you have an internet connection.</p>\n<p><strong>STORAGE MEASUREMENTS — FROM BITS TO TERABYTES</strong></p>\n<p>One of the most confusing things for JSS 1 students is the measurement of storage. Terms like megabyte, gigabyte, and terabyte get thrown around a lot, but what do they actually mean?</p>\n<p>Let us start from the very beginning.</p>\n<p><strong>THE BIT — THE SMALLEST UNIT</strong></p>\n<p>A bit (short for binary digit) is the smallest unit of data in a computer. It can only have one of two values: 0 or 1. Everything a computer does — every photo, every song, every word you type — ultimately comes down to combinations of 0s and 1s.</p>\n<p>1 bit = either a 0 or a 1</p>\n<p><strong>THE BYTE — BUILDING BLOCKS OF DATA</strong></p>\n<p>A byte is made up of 8 bits. One byte can represent a single character — for example, the letter \"A\" or the number \"5.\"</p>\n<p>1 Byte = 8 Bits</p>\n<h2>FROM BYTES TO BIGGER UNITS</h2>\n<p>As we go higher, each unit is roughly 1,000 times larger than the one before it (though technically, computers use 1,024 as the multiplier in binary):</p>\n<p>Kilobyte (KB): 1 KB = 1,024 Bytes</p>\n<p>A very short text document (like a brief note) is about 1 KB.</p>\n<p>Megabyte (MB): 1 MB = 1,024 KB</p>\n<p>A typical Nigerian passport photograph in digital form is about 200KB–500KB. A 3-minute MP3 song is roughly 3–5MB.</p>\n<p>Gigabyte (GB): 1 GB = 1,024 MB</p>\n<p>A full-length Nollywood movie in HD quality is typically 1–2GB. Most smartphones today come with 32GB, 64GB, or 128GB of internal storage.</p>\n<p>Terabyte (TB): 1 TB = 1,024 GB</p>\n<p>Hard disks for desktop computers commonly come in 1TB or 2TB sizes. Large organisations like banks and government agencies store thousands of terabytes of data.</p>\n<p>Petabyte (PB): 1 PB = 1,024 TB</p>\n<p>This is the level where very large companies and data centres operate. You do not need to worry about this for now, but it is good to know it exists.</p>\n<h2>QUICK MEMORY TRICK:</h2>\n<p>Bit → Byte → Kilobyte → Megabyte → Gigabyte → Terabyte</p>\n<p>\"Big Boys Know More Good Things\"</p>\n<h2>PRACTICAL APPLICATIONS IN EVERYDAY NIGERIAN LIFE</h2>\n<p>You interact with storage every single day, even if you do not notice it. Here are some practical Nigerian examples:</p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p><strong>JAMB CBT Centres: </strong>Every JAMB Computer-Based Test centre in Nigeria relies heavily on storage devices. The exam questions are stored on servers (hard disks), and students' answers are recorded and stored during the exam.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Phone Storage:</strong> When your Android phone shows \"Storage Full, cannot take photo,\" that is your phone's internal flash storage telling you it has reached its limit. This is secondary storage in action.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Cyber Cafes:</strong> In many Nigerian cities and towns, cyber cafes use computers with large hard disks to store software and client files. Attendants often use flash drives to transfer files for customers.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>School Computer Labs: </strong>JSS and SSS students use flash drives to save their practical assignments and take them home. This is the most direct secondary storage experience most Nigerian students have.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Banking Systems: </strong>Nigerian banks like Access Bank, GTBank, and First Bank store millions of customer records and transaction histories on large hard disk arrays and cloud storage systems. Without reliable storage, banking in Nigeria would be impossible.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Music and Video Downloads:</strong> When you download a Davido or Burna Boy song to your phone so you can play it without internet, you are saving it to your phone's secondary storage — either internal storage or an SD card.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h2>ADVANTAGES AND DISADVANTAGES OF STORAGE DEVICES</h2>\n<p><strong>RAM - </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Advantages</strong>: Very fast access speed; allows the computer to multitask smoothly</p>\n<p><strong>Disadvantages</strong>: Loses all data when power goes off; expensive relative to size</p>\n<h2>ROM -</h2>\n<p><strong>Advantages</strong>: Reliable; non-volatile; protects critical startup instructions</p>\n<p><strong>Disadvantages</strong>: Cannot be updated or modified easily; small capacity</p>\n<p><strong>Hard Disk Drive (HDD)</strong></p>\n<p>Advantages: Large storage capacity; relatively affordable; stores data permanently</p>\n<p>Disadvantages: Slower than RAM or SSD; mechanical parts can fail; heavier</p>\n<p><strong>Flash Drive</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Advantages</strong>: Very portable; plug-and-play; no external power needed; durable</p>\n<p><strong>Disadvantages</strong>: Small enough to misplace easily; can be lost or stolen; slower than SSD</p>\n<p><strong>SSD</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Advantages</strong>: Very fast; durable (no moving parts); quiet; energy-efficient</p>\n<p><strong>Disadvantages</strong>: More expensive than HDD; limited write cycles</p>\n<h2>SAFETY AND ETHICAL CONSIDERATIONS</h2>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p><strong>Safe Handling of Storage Devices: </strong>Always remove your flash drive properly using the \"Safely Remove Hardware\" option on Windows. Pulling it out abruptly can corrupt your files.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Virus and Malware Awareness: </strong>Flash drives are one of the most common ways computer viruses spread in Nigeria. Always scan any USB device with antivirus software (like Avast or Windows Defender) before opening files on a school or shared computer.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Data Privacy: </strong>Be careful what you store on removable devices. If you lose a flash drive containing personal information, examination materials, or private photos, someone else could access that data. Avoid storing sensitive information on devices you carry around carelessly.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Responsible Sharing:</strong> Do not copy and share software, movies, or music from other people's flash drives without permission. This is a form of digital theft called software piracy, and it is illegal.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Backup Your Data:</strong> Nigerian students should form the habit of keeping backup copies of important school work — save your assignment in at least two places (e.g., your flash drive and your phone's cloud backup). You never know when NEPA or a virus will strike.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h2>CLASSROOM AND HOME ACTIVITIES</h2>\n<p><strong>ACTIVITY 1 — LABEL THE STORAGE DEVICE</strong></p>\n<p>Draw a desktop computer and label where the RAM, ROM, and hard disk are located. Then draw a flash drive and a memory card separately. Write one function beside each component.</p>\n<p><strong>ACTIVITY 2 — STORAGE SCAVENGER HUNT</strong></p>\n<p>Look around your home or school and list five devices that use storage. For each device, identify whether it uses primary storage, secondary storage, or both. Examples to look for: smartphones, tablets, cameras, school computers, smart TVs.</p>\n<p><strong>ACTIVITY 3 — CONVERSION CHALLENGE</strong></p>\n<p>Without a calculator, convert the following:</p>\n<p>a) How many bytes are in 2 kilobytes?</p>\n<p>b) How many kilobytes are in 1 megabyte?</p>\n<p>c) If a flash drive holds 8GB, how many 4MB songs can it store?</p>\n<p><em><strong>(Hint: Divide 8,000MB by 4MB per song)</strong></em></p>\n<p><strong>ACTIVITY 4 — RAM VS ROM DEBATE</strong></p>\n<p>Divide your class into two groups. One group argues that RAM is more important than ROM. The other argues ROM is more important. After 5 minutes of discussion, your teacher will ask each group to present their strongest point. There is no wrong answer — this activity is about understanding the role of each.</p>\n<h2>ASSESSMENT QUESTIONS</h2>\n<h2>OBJECTIVE QUESTIONS</h2>\n<p>1. Which of the following is a PRIMARY storage device?</p>\n<p>A. Hard Disk Drive</p>\n<p>B. Flash Drive</p>\n<p>C. RAM</p>\n<p>D. DVD</p>\n<p>2. ROM stands for:</p>\n<p>A. Random Output Memory</p>\n<p>B. Read-Only Memory</p>\n<p>C. Random-Only Memory</p>\n<p>D. Read-Open Module</p>\n<p>3. A student saves a 5MB document on a 1GB flash drive. Approximately how many similar documents can the flash drive hold?</p>\n<p>A. 50</p>\n<p>B. 100</p>\n<p>C. 200</p>\n<p>D. 500</p>\n<p>4. Which type of storage loses its data when the computer is switched off?</p>\n<p>A. ROM</p>\n<p>B. Hard Disk</p>\n<p>C. Flash Drive</p>\n<p>D. RAM</p>\n<p>5. Which of the following is the correct order of storage units from smallest to largest?</p>\n<p>A. Byte, Bit, KB, MB, GB, TB</p>\n<p>B. Bit, Byte, KB, MB, GB, TB</p>\n<p>C. KB, MB, GB, TB, Bit, Byte</p>\n<p>D. Bit, Byte, MB, KB, GB, TB</p>\n<h2>THEORY QUESTIONS</h2>\n<p>1. Explain the difference between RAM and ROM. In your answer, mention at least two features of each and give one real-life example to illustrate either type.</p>\n<p>2. A JSS 2 student transfers her class assignment using a flash drive from the school computer lab to her home laptop.</p>\n<p>(a) What type of storage is the flash drive?</p>\n<p>(b) When she opens the assignment on the home laptop, which part of the computer processes and displays it?</p>\n<p>(c) What might happen if the home laptop suddenly loses power while she is editing the document?</p>\n<p>3. Describe THREE different secondary storage devices. For each device, state one advantage and one disadvantage, and give one example of how it is used in Nigeria.</p>\n<h2>SUMMARY</h2>\n<p>Let us quickly recap the key things we covered in this lesson:</p>\n<p>- A storage unit is a hardware component used to store data, instructions, or information in a computer.</p>\n<p>- Storage is divided into two categories: Primary Storage (RAM and ROM) and Secondary Storage (hard disk, flash drive, memory card, etc.).</p>\n<p>- RAM is volatile, fast, and temporary — it loses data when power goes off.</p>\n<p>- ROM is non-volatile and permanent — it holds the computer's startup instructions.</p>\n<p>- Secondary storage holds data permanently and includes hard disks, SSDs, flash drives, memory cards, and optical discs.</p>\n<p>- Storage is measured in units starting from the smallest (bit) all the way up to terabytes and beyond: <strong>Bit → Byte → KB → MB → GB → TB.</strong></p>\n<p>- 1 Byte = 8 Bits; 1 KB = 1,024 Bytes; 1 MB = 1,024 KB; 1 GB = 1,024 MB; 1 TB = 1,024 GB.</p>\n<h2>CONCLUSION</h2>\n<p>Understanding computer storage is not just about passing your JSS 1 examination — it is about understanding the digital world you live in. Every time you save a file, copy music to your phone, or submit an assignment in the computer lab, storage is doing the work behind the scenes.</p>\n<p>As technology advances in Nigeria — from the expansion of CBT examination centres across the country to the growth of digital banking and e-learning platforms — knowledge of storage devices becomes increasingly valuable. Students who understand these concepts today are better prepared to work with computers tomorrow, whether as engineers, bankers, teachers, or tech entrepreneurs.</p>\n<p>Make it a habit to always save your work, back it up in more than one place, and handle storage devices responsibly. Your future self will thank you.</p>\n<h2>FREQUENTLY ASKED QUESTIONS (FAQ)</h2>\n<p><strong>1. What is the difference between storage and memory in a computer?</strong></p>\n<p>A: In everyday language, people often use \"memory\" and \"storage\" to mean the same thing — but in computing, they are different. Memory (like RAM) is temporary working space used while the computer is on. Storage (like a hard disk or flash drive) is where data is kept permanently, even after the computer is switched off.</p>\n<p><strong>2. Why does my phone say \"storage full\" when I still have free space in my apps?</strong></p>\n<p>A: This usually happens because your phone has two types of storage — internal storage and app memory. Even if your apps section shows space, your media storage (photos, music, downloads) may be full. Transferring files to an SD card or deleting unused downloads can solve this problem.</p>\n<p><strong>3. Is a flash drive the same as RAM?</strong></p>\n<p>A: No. A flash drive is secondary storage — it holds files permanently and is removable. RAM is primary storage — it is built into the computer, is temporary, and only works while the computer is running. They serve completely different purposes.</p>\n<p><strong>4. How many GB do I need for a school laptop?</strong></p>\n<p>A: For a JSS or SSS student in Nigeria, a laptop with at least 4GB of RAM and 256GB of storage (HDD or SSD) is generally sufficient for school work, basic internet browsing, and running applications like Microsoft Office. The more RAM, the smoother the experience when multitasking.</p>\n<p><strong>5. Can viruses destroy data on a hard disk?</strong></p>\n<p>A: Yes. Some viruses and malware can delete, corrupt, or encrypt your files, making them inaccessible. This is why it is important to install antivirus software, avoid downloading files from unknown sources, and always back up important data. In Nigeria, this is especially relevant because flash drives are frequently shared between computers, making virus transfer more common.</p>\n<p><strong>6. What is the largest storage unit?</strong></p>\n<p>A: For everyday purposes, the Terabyte (TB) is the largest you will commonly encounter. However, in computing and data science, there are larger units: Petabyte (PB), Exabyte (EB), Zettabyte (ZB), and even Yottabyte (YB). These are used by major tech companies and governments to describe massive amounts of data stored in data centres.</p>\n<p><strong>KEYWORDS: <em>computer storage units, primary storage, secondary storage, RAM, ROM, hard disk, flash drive, storage measurements, bit, byte, megabyte, gigabyte, terabyte, JSS 1 digital technology, NERDC curriculum, computer hardware components</em></strong></p>", "<p><strong>INTRODUCTION: </strong><em>YOUR PHONE DOES MORE THAN YOU THINK</em></p>\n<p>Every computer, phone, and tablet in Nigeria — from the shared desktop in your school computer lab to the Tecno or Infinix phone in your pocket — runs on software. Without it, even the most expensive laptop would just sit there doing absolutely nothing. It would be like buying a brand-new generator and never putting fuel in it.</p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/img/image53.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\"></p>\n<p>In this lesson, we are going to break down exactly what software means, explore its two main types, and take a close look at three very important examples: the operating system, the word processor, and the web browser. Everything will be explained in simple language with real-life Nigerian examples, so you can connect what you study in class to what you already experience every day.</p>\n<p>By the time you finish reading, software will no longer feel like a mysterious concept — it will feel like something you already know.</p>\n<h2>LEARNING OBJECTIVES</h2>\n<p>By the end of this lesson, students should be able to:</p>\n<p>1. Define the term \"software\" and explain its role in a computer system.</p>\n<p>2. Distinguish between system software and application software with clear examples.</p>\n<p>3. Identify and explain at least five functions of an operating system.</p>\n<p>4. Describe the purpose of word processing software and name practical examples.</p>\n<p>5. Explain what a web browser is and identify at least two examples commonly used in Nigeria.</p>\n<p>6. Appreciate the importance of software in everyday digital life in Nigeria.</p>\n<p><strong>THE SOFTWARE — UNDERSTANDING THE BASICS</strong></p>\n<p>Before we go any further, let us nail down the meaning of software properly. A computer system has two main parts: hardware and software. Hardware refers to the physical components you can see and touch — the screen, the keyboard, the CPU, the mouse, and the circuit boards inside the machine. Software, on the other hand, is the set of instructions and programs that tell all that hardware what to do.</p>\n<p>Put simply: hardware is the body of the computer. Software is the brain.</p>\n<p>Here is a helpful way to think about it. Imagine a DVD player sitting on a shelf. The player itself — the plastic casing, the disc tray, the buttons — is the hardware. The movie stored on the DVD disc that tells the player what to display on your screen is more like software — the content and instructions that give the hardware a purpose and make it useful.</p>\n<h2>DEFINITION OF SOFTWARE</h2>\n<p><em>Software is a collection of programs, data, and instructions that enable a computer to perform specific tasks. It serves as the bridge between the user and the computer hardware, allowing humans to communicate with machines in a way the machines can understand and act upon.</em></p>\n<p>Software is broadly divided into two major categories:</p>\n<p><strong>- System Software</strong></p>\n<p><strong>- Application Software</strong></p>\n<p>Let us look at each one in detail.</p>\n<p><strong>SYSTEM SOFTWARE — THE ENGINE RUNNING BEHIND THE SCENES</strong></p>\n<p>System software is the type of software that manages, controls, and coordinates the computer's hardware so that all other software can function properly. Most of the time, users do not interact with system software directly. It operates quietly in the background, making sure everything is running smoothly.</p>\n<p>A good way to picture this: think of a traffic warden at a busy junction in Lagos. Drivers and vehicles come and go, but the traffic warden is the one keeping everything organised, preventing collisions, and making sure movement flows without chaos. Without the traffic warden, everything grinds to a halt. System software plays exactly that role inside your computer.</p>\n<p><strong>Types of System Software</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Operating System</strong> — the most important type of system software. It manages all other programs and controls the hardware directly. We will cover this in great detail shortly.</p>\n<p><strong>Device Drivers</strong> — small but essential programs that help the computer communicate with external devices like printers, USB flash drives, webcams, and speakers. When you plug a printer into a school computer and it starts working automatically, a device driver made that happen.</p>\n<p><strong>Utility Programs</strong> — maintenance tools that keep the computer healthy and running well. Examples include antivirus software like Avast or Windows Defender, disk cleaners, and file compression tools like WinRAR or 7-Zip.</p>\n<p><strong>Language Translators </strong>— programs that convert code written by programmers into a language the computer processor can understand. Examples include compilers and interpreters.</p>\n<p><strong>THE OPERATING SYSTEM — THE MOST IMPORTANT SOFTWARE ON ANY DEVICE</strong></p>\n<p>If there is one piece of software every student must understand well, it is the operating system. The operating system — commonly abbreviated as OS — is the master program that loads the moment you turn on any computing device. It stays active the entire time the device is on and shuts down only when you power off.</p>\n<p>Everything else — your apps, your files, your internet connection, your screen display — depends entirely on the operating system to work.</p>\n<p>You have already used an operating system, even if you never called it that. If you have typed a document on a Windows laptop, used an Android phone, or played games on a phone or computer, you have used an operating system.</p>\n<h2>COMMON EXAMPLES OF OPERATING SYSTEMS</h2>\n<p>Windows 10 and Windows 11 — the operating system found on most laptops and desktop computers in Nigerian schools, offices, and homes.            </p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 100%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><img src=\"assets/img/image54.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\"></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Windows File Explorer gif</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Android</strong> — the OS that powers the vast majority of smartphones in Nigeria, including Tecno, Infinix, Samsung Galaxy, and others.</p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/img/image55.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\"></p>\n<p><strong>iOS</strong> — the operating system used on Apple iPhones and iPads.</p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/img/image56.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\"></p>\n<p><strong>macOS</strong> — found on Apple MacBook laptops and iMac desktop computers.</p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/img/image57.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\"></p>\n<p><strong>Linux</strong> — a free and open-source OS used by software developers, researchers, and some government IT systems.</p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/img/image58.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\"></p>\n<p><strong>Chrome OS</strong> — found on Chromebook laptops, increasingly used in some schools for affordability.</p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/img/image59.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\"></p>\n<h2>FUNCTIONS OF AN OPERATING SYSTEM</h2>\n<p>The operating system does far more than just start your computer. Here are its seven key functions, explained with real-life examples:</p>\n<h2>1. PROCESS MANAGEMENT</h2>\n<p>A modern computer handles many tasks at the same time. Right now, as you read this, your phone might be downloading an update, playing background music, and running WhatsApp — all at once. The OS manages all these running processes, deciding which one gets access to the processor at any given moment, and making sure they do not interfere with each other. This is called multitasking, and your OS is the one making it possible.</p>\n<h2>2. MEMORY MANAGEMENT</h2>\n<p>RAM — Random Access Memory — is the short-term working memory of a computer. It is where all active programs and data are temporarily stored while in use. RAM is limited, and the OS is responsible for allocating the right amount of memory to each running program. When you open too many apps on your phone and it starts lagging or freezing, that is the OS struggling to manage a full RAM.</p>\n<h2>3. FILE MANAGEMENT</h2>\n<p>Every document, photo, video, and app on your device is stored as a file. The OS organises all these files into a structured system of folders and directories, making it easy to save, find, rename, move, copy, and delete them. When you create a new folder on your desktop and drag documents into it, the OS file management system is doing that work.</p>\n<h2>4. DEVICE MANAGEMENT</h2>\n<p>Your computer interacts with many devices — the keyboard sends input, the monitor displays output, the printer produces hard copies, and flash drives transfer files. The OS manages all communication between the computer and these input and output devices. Without this function, plugging in a flash drive would do absolutely nothing.</p>\n<h2>5. USER INTERFACE</h2>\n<p>The user interface is what you see and interact with on screen. The desktop icons, taskbar, start menu, folders, and windows on a Windows computer are all provided by the OS. Without a user interface, you would have to type complex technical commands just to open a simple document. The OS makes computing accessible to everyone, not just technical experts.</p>\n<h2>6. SECURITY AND ACCESS CONTROL</h2>\n<p>The OS protects your device and data by managing user accounts, passwords, and access permissions. This is why school computer labs often have restricted student accounts that cannot install software or access certain folders. The OS enforces those limits. It also works alongside antivirus software to monitor for suspicious activity.</p>\n<h2>7. ERROR DETECTION AND HANDLING</h2>\n<p>When a program crashes or something goes wrong, the OS detects the problem and responds. Rather than letting the entire system collapse, it isolates the affected program, displays an error message, and attempts to recover. The familiar \"This app has stopped working\" notification on Android is a classic example of the OS catching and reporting an error.</p>\n<p><strong>APPLICATION SOFTWARE — THE TOOLS YOU USE EVERY SINGLE DAY</strong></p>\n<p>While system software works silently in the background, application software is what most users interact with directly. Application software — often called simply apps or programs — is designed to help users accomplish specific, real-world tasks.</p>\n<p>Every time you type an assignment, browse the internet, watch a video, play a game, or video call a friend, you are using application software.</p>\n<p><strong><u>Examples of Application Software Categories</u></strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Word Processing Software — for typing, editing, and formatting documents.</p></li>\n<li><p>Web Browsers — for accessing websites and the internet.</p></li>\n<li><p>Spreadsheet Software — for calculations, tables, and data analysis. Examples: Microsoft Excel, Google Sheets.</p></li>\n<li><p>Presentation Software — for creating visual slide presentations. Examples: Microsoft PowerPoint, Google Slides.</p></li>\n<li><p>Media Players — for playing audio and video files. Example: VLC Media Player.</p></li>\n<li><p>Communication Apps — for messaging, calls, and video meetings. Examples: WhatsApp, Zoom, Google Meet.</p></li>\n<li><p>Educational Apps — software designed specifically for learning. Examples: uLesson, Passnownow, Khan Academy.</p></li>\n<li><p>Graphics Software — for designing images and visual content. Examples: Canva, Adobe Photoshop.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Now let us take a closer look at two of the most important types of application software for students: word processors and web browsers.</p>\n<p><strong>WORD PROCESSORS — YOUR DIGITAL WRITING TOOL</strong></p>\n<p>A word processor is application software used to create, edit, format, save, and print text-based documents. It is like a highly advanced typewriter, but with capabilities no typewriter could ever match.</p>\n<p>Before word processors became common, typists used physical typewriters to produce documents. Mistakes meant starting over or reaching for correction fluid. Word processors changed everything — you can now write, delete, rearrange, and format text freely, and the document will still look clean and professional every time.</p>\n<p>Popular Word Processing Software</p>\n<p><strong>Microsoft Word</strong> — the most widely used word processor in Nigerian schools and workplaces. Most Digital Technology practicals and typed assignments are done in Microsoft Word.</p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/img/image60.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\"></p>\n<p><strong>Google Docs</strong> — a free, browser-based word processor by Google. It works without installing any software and allows multiple people to edit the same document at the same time. Popular among students with internet access.</p>\n<p><strong>LibreOffice Writer</strong> — a completely free alternative to Microsoft Word. Used by many Nigerian government institutions and schools that cannot afford Microsoft Office licences.</p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/img/image61.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\"></p>\n<p><strong>Notepad and WordPad</strong> — basic text tools that come pre-installed with Windows. Useful for quick notes but lack the formatting power of Word or Google Docs.</p>\n<p><strong>What You Can Do with a Word Processor</strong></p>\n<p>- Type letters, essays, assignments, reports, and CV documents.</p>\n<p>- Change font style, size, and colour.</p>\n<p>- Bold, italicise, or underline important text.</p>\n<p>- Use automatic spelling and grammar checking.</p>\n<p>- Insert tables, images, and page numbers into documents.</p>\n<p>- Save files in different formats including .docx and .pdf.</p>\n<p>- Print documents directly from the software.</p>\n<p>- Collaborate with others in real time when using Google Docs.</p>\n<p>Nigerian Context: In secondary schools across Nigeria, students regularly use Microsoft Word to type Digital Technology practicals and submit assignments. Many WAEC and NECO Digital Technology practical examinations require candidates to demonstrate word processing skills. Mastering a word processor early gives any Nigerian student a real academic and professional advantage.</p>\n<p><strong>WEB BROWSERS — YOUR GATEWAY TO THE INTERNET</strong></p>\n<p>A web browser is application software that allows users to access, navigate, and view content on the World Wide Web. Simply put, it is the program you open when you want to visit a website.</p>\n<p>When you type a web address like www.google.com into a browser, the browser sends a request to the web server hosting that website. The server responds by sending back the page's content, and the browser displays it on your screen — with text, images, videos, and everything else.</p>\n<p><strong>Popular Web Browsers</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p><strong>Google Chrome</strong> — the most popular browser in Nigeria and globally. Known for its speed and wide compatibility. Used on most school and office computers in Nigeria.</p></li>\n<li><p><img src=\"assets/img/image62.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\"></p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Opera Mini</strong> — extremely popular in Nigeria, especially on mobile phones, because of its built-in data compression feature. It reduces the amount of data a webpage consumes, which is very helpful for users on limited data plans.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Mozilla Firefox</strong> — a trusted, privacy-focused browser. A solid alternative to Chrome for users who are concerned about how their data is handled online.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Microsoft Edge</strong> — pre-installed on all Windows 10 and Windows 11 computers. Faster and more modern than the old Internet Explorer it replaced.<br />\n<img src=\"assets/img/image63.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\"></p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Safari</strong> — the default browser on all Apple devices, including iPhones, iPads, and MacBooks.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>UC Browser</strong> — widely used on lower-end Android phones in Nigeria because of its light data consumption.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h2>KEY FEATURES OF A WEB BROWSER</h2>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p><strong>Address Bar</strong> — where you type a website's URL to navigate directly to it.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Tabs</strong> — allow you to open and switch between multiple websites in the same window.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Bookmarks</strong> — save the addresses of websites you visit frequently so you do not have to retype them each time.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>History</strong> — a log of every website you have visited, which you can search or clear.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Download Manager</strong> — lets you save files such as documents, images, and videos from the internet to your device.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Private or Incognito Mode</strong> — browse without the browser saving your history, cookies, or form data.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Extensions and Add-ons</strong> — small tools you can install in your browser to add extra features, such as ad blockers or grammar checkers.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>SYSTEM SOFTWARE VS APPLICATION SOFTWARE — A QUICK COMPARISON</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Feature System Software Application Software</strong></p>\n<p>Purpose Manages hardware and resources Performs specific user tasks</p>\n<p>User Interaction Works mostly in the background Directly used by the user</p>\n<p>Dependency Runs independently and loads first Depends on system software to run</p>\n<p>Examples Windows, Android, Linux Word, Chrome, WhatsApp, Excel</p>\n<p>Installation Pre-installed or required at startup Installed by the user as needed</p>\n<p>Runs First Yes, before anything else No, needs the OS already running</p>\n<h2>PRACTICAL APPLICATIONS IN EVERYDAY NIGERIAN LIFE</h2>\n<p>Understanding software is not just an exam topic. It connects directly to life around you every single day.</p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p><strong>In your school computer lab</strong>: When the teacher asks you to open Microsoft Word, you are running application software on top of system software. Windows had to load first before Word could even open.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>At a cybercafe in your neighbourhood</strong>: When the operator reinstalls Windows on a crashed machine, they are working with system software. When customers come in to browse, type, or print, they are using application software.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>On your Android phone</strong>: Every app you download from Google Play Store is application software. Android itself is the system software making all of it possible.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>In Nigerian government offices</strong>: The IPPIS system used to manage federal civil servant salaries is application software running on a server operating system.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>During JAMB CBT examinations</strong>: The examination interface students interact with at CBT centres is application software. The computers running it depend on Windows or another OS as their foundation.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h2>ADVANTAGES AND DISADVANTAGES OF SOFTWARE</h2>\n<h2>ADVANTAGES</h2>\n<p>i. Software automates repetitive tasks and saves enormous amounts of time and effort.</p>\n<p>ii. It enables instant communication across any distance — WhatsApp and Zoom connect Nigerians locally and internationally every day.</p>\n<p>iii. Educational software like uLesson and Passnownow gives Nigerian students access to quality study materials from home.</p>\n<p>iv. Word processors produce professional-looking documents far faster than handwriting ever could.</p>\n<p>v. Operating systems allow multiple programs to share hardware resources efficiently, making multitasking possible.</p>\n<p>vi. Software can be updated remotely to fix bugs and add new features without replacing any hardware.</p>\n<h2>DISADVANTAGES</h2>\n<p>i. Some software is expensive to purchase and licence. Microsoft Office, for example, requires a paid subscription.</p>\n<p>ii. Software can contain bugs — programming errors that cause crashes, freezes, or unexpected data loss.</p>\n<p>iii. Many applications require an active internet connection, which remains a challenge in parts of Nigeria with poor network coverage.</p>\n<p>iv. Malicious software — including viruses, malware, and ransomware — can destroy files, steal personal data, and damage systems.</p>\n<p>v. Frequent updates consume mobile data and can noticeably slow down older, low-specification devices.</p>\n<p>vi. Software piracy is widespread in Nigeria, which exposes users to serious security risks and potential legal consequences.</p>\n<h2>DIGITAL SAFETY AND RESPONSIBLE USE OF SOFTWARE</h2>\n<p>Knowing how to use software also means taking responsibility for using it safely and ethically. Here are important digital safety rules every JSS 1 student should know and follow:</p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p>Download software only from trusted sources. Stick to official platforms like the Google Play Store, the Apple App Store, or official developer websites. Apps from unknown sources frequently contain hidden malware.</p></li>\n<li><p>Keep your operating system and apps updated. Updates regularly patch security vulnerabilities that cybercriminals exploit to gain access to devices.</p></li>\n<li><p>Use strong passwords to secure your accounts on browsers, educational platforms, and communication apps. Never use obvious passwords like your date of birth or a simple sequence like 12345.</p></li>\n<li><p>Never pirate software. Illegally copying or distributing software is against Nigerian law and international copyright agreements. It also exposes your device to infected and corrupted files.</p></li>\n<li><p>Think before you click. Not every website, pop-up advertisement, or download link is safe or honest. Be especially careful when browsing on public Wi-Fi or using a shared computer.</p></li>\n<li><p>Respect others' digital privacy. Do not access, modify, or share someone else's files or accounts without their knowledge and permission.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h2>CLASSROOM AND HOME ACTIVITIES</h2>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p><strong>ACTIVITY 1 — SOFTWARE SORTING GAME</strong></p></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Your teacher will call out the names of different software programs one by one. Students must immediately respond by calling out either \"System Software\" or \"Application Software.\" Use these examples to play: Windows 11, WhatsApp, Android, Microsoft Word, Linux, VLC Media Player, Google Chrome, iOS, Avast Antivirus, Google Docs.</p>\n<ol start=\"2\" type=\"1\">\n<li><p><strong>ACTIVITY 2 — OS IDENTIFICATION EXERCISE</strong></p></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Look at three different devices available to you — a school desktop, a mobile phone, and a tablet or another laptop. For each device, write down the name of the operating system running on it and one function the OS performs on that device. Compare your answers with a classmate afterwards.</p>\n<ol start=\"3\" type=\"1\">\n<li><p><strong>ACTIVITY 3 — WORD PROCESSOR PRACTICE</strong></p></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Open Microsoft Word or Google Docs. Type a short paragraph about yourself — include your name, your school, your class, and your favourite subject. Change the font to Times New Roman, size 14. Make your name bold. Save the file as \"About Me.\" If a printer is available, print it out.</p>\n<ol start=\"4\" type=\"1\">\n<li><p><strong>ACTIVITY 4 — BROWSER EXPLORATION</strong></p></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Open any web browser and go to www.google.com. Search for \"JSS 1 Digital Technology Nigeria.\" Write down the names of three websites that appear in the results. For each one, write a sentence describing what it seems to be about based on the title and short description shown.</p>\n<h2>ASSESSMENT QUESTIONS</h2>\n<p><strong>SECTION A — OBJECTIVE QUESTIONS</strong></p>\n<p>1. Which of the following is the best example of system software?</p>\n<p>A. Microsoft Word</p>\n<p>B. VLC Media Player</p>\n<p>C. Windows 11</p>\n<p>D. Google Chrome</p>\n<p>2. The function of an operating system that controls how programs share RAM is called:</p>\n<p>A. File Management</p>\n<p>B. Device Management</p>\n<p>C. Memory Management</p>\n<p>D. User Interface</p>\n<p>3. Opera Mini is widely used in Nigeria on mobile phones mainly because:</p>\n<p>A. It was developed by a Nigerian company</p>\n<p>B. It compresses data and reduces internet usage</p>\n<p>C. It is the only browser that works on Android</p>\n<p>D. It has the best visual design among all browsers</p>\n<p>4. Which of the following is NOT a function of an operating system?</p>\n<p>A. Managing running programs</p>\n<p>B. Browsing websites on the internet</p>\n<p>C. Organising files into folders</p>\n<p>D. Providing a user interface</p>\n<p>5. Google Docs is best described as:</p>\n<p>A. An operating system</p>\n<p>B. System software</p>\n<p>C. A cloud-based word processing application</p>\n<p>D. A device driver</p>\n<p><strong>SECTION B — THEORY QUESTIONS</strong></p>\n<p>1. Define software and clearly explain the difference between system software and application software. Give two examples of each type.</p>\n<p>2. List and explain FIVE functions of an operating system. For each function, provide a real-life example that a Nigerian student can relate to.</p>\n<p>3. What is a word processor? State THREE features that make word processors useful for secondary school students in Nigeria. Name two examples of word processing software.</p>\n<h2>SUMMARY</h2>\n<p>Software is a set of programs and instructions that tells computer hardware what to do. You cannot touch it, but it is essential for any computing device to function. There are two main types: system software, which manages hardware and creates the environment for other programs to run, and application software, which performs specific tasks for the user.</p>\n<p>The operating system is the most important type of system software. It controls all hardware and software resources on a device. The seven key functions of an operating system are process management, memory management, file management, device management, user interface, security and access control, and error detection and handling.</p>\n<p>Common operating systems include Windows, Android, iOS, macOS, and Linux.</p>\n<p>A word processor is application software used to create, edit, format, and print text documents. Key examples are Microsoft Word, Google Docs, and LibreOffice Writer. A web browser is application software used to access websites and navigate the internet. Popular examples in Nigeria include Google Chrome, Opera Mini, Mozilla Firefox, and Microsoft Edge.</p>\n<h2>CONCLUSION</h2>\n<p>Software is truly the soul of every computing device. From the Android operating system managing your Tecno phone to the Google Chrome browser you open to research homework answers, software is what transforms a collection of electronic parts into a tool that genuinely changes lives.</p>\n<p>As a JSS 1 student in Nigeria, understanding what software is, how it is categorised, and what specific programs like operating systems, word processors, and web browsers actually do gives you a powerful foundation — not just for passing your Digital Technology exams, but for navigating the digital world with real confidence.</p>\n<p>Technology continues to evolve rapidly, and so does software. New operating systems are released, browsers improve, and word processors gain powerful new features every year. But the core concepts you have learned in this lesson will remain relevant no matter how much the technology around you changes. The student who understands the fundamentals will always be better prepared to adapt, grow, and succeed. Keep practicing. Keep exploring. And never stop asking how the technology around you really works.</p>\n<h2>FREQUENTLY ASKED QUESTIONS</h2>\n<p><strong>1. What is the simplest definition of software for a JSS student?</strong></p>\n<p>Software is a set of instructions or programs that tells a computer what to do. Unlike hardware — the physical parts you can see and touch — software is invisible. But without it, no computing device can function at all.</p>\n<p><strong>2. What is the difference between system software and application software?</strong></p>\n<p>System software manages and controls the computer's hardware and creates the foundation for all other programs to run. It works mostly in the background. Application software is what the user directly interacts with to perform specific tasks — like typing a document or browsing the internet. System software always runs first, and application software depends on it.</p>\n<p><strong>3. What are the main functions of an operating system?</strong></p>\n<p>The OS performs seven major functions: managing all running programs, controlling how RAM is shared among programs, organising files and folders, handling communication with devices like printers and flash drives, providing the visual desktop environment, managing passwords and access permissions, and detecting and reporting system errors.</p>\n<p><strong>4. Which web browser is most suitable for Nigerian students?</strong></p>\n<p>Google Chrome is the most widely supported browser and is ideal for schoolwork. However, Opera Mini is the top choice for students on limited data plans because it compresses web pages significantly. Mozilla Firefox is a strong option for students who are concerned about online privacy.</p>\n<p><strong>5. Is WhatsApp system software or application software?</strong></p>\n<p>WhatsApp is application software. It is an app installed on your phone to perform a specific task — messaging and calling. The operating system running underneath it, such as Android, is the system software that makes it possible for WhatsApp to function.</p>\n<p><strong>6. Can a computer work without an operating system?</strong></p>\n<p>No, not in any practical sense. Without an operating system, the hardware has no instructions on how to operate. You could not open any apps, see a display, use a keyboard, or do anything useful at all. The operating system is what turns a collection of electronic components into a working, usable computer.</p>", "<p><img src=\"assets/img/image64.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\"><strong>INTRODUCTION:</strong></p>\n<p>Think about the last time the lights went out suddenly in your house while you were watching TV — the screen just cut off without warning. Now imagine that happening to a computer while you are working on a school assignment. Everything you have been typing could be lost in an instant.</p>\n<p>That situation right there is exactly why knowing how to properly start and shut down a computer is one of the first and most important skills every student needs to learn. It is not just about pressing a button and walking away. There is a right way and a wrong way to do it, and the difference matters more than most people realize.</p>\n<p>In homes, schools, and cyber cafes all across Nigeria — from Lagos Island to Kano city — people use computers every day. But many students and even adults do not know the correct process for turning a computer on or off safely. This lesson will change that.</p>\n<p>By the time you finish reading this, you will understand what booting a computer means, the steps involved in starting and shutting down a system, and why handling your device with care is not optional — it is necessary.</p>\n\n<h2>LEARNING OBJECTIVES</h2>\n<p>By the end of this lesson, JSS 1 students should be able to:</p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><blockquote>\n<p>Define the term \"booting\" in relation to computer systems</p>\n</blockquote></li>\n<li><blockquote>\n<p>Identify and explain the two main types of booting</p>\n</blockquote></li>\n<li><blockquote>\n<p>Describe the correct steps involved in booting a computer</p>\n</blockquote></li>\n<li><blockquote>\n<p>Demonstrate the proper procedure for shutting down a computer</p>\n</blockquote></li>\n<li><blockquote>\n<p>Explain the importance of safe device handling</p>\n</blockquote></li>\n<li><blockquote>\n<p>Identify harmful habits that can damage a computer system</p>\n</blockquote></li>\n</ol>\n<h2>BOOTING A PC</h2>\n<p>The word \"booting\" might sound unusual, but it simply means <em><strong>the process of starting up a computer</strong></em>. When you switch on a computer, it does not just wake up like a person jumping out of bed. It goes through a series of internal checks and loading steps before it is ready for you to use.</p>\n<p>The full process is called <em><strong>booting</strong></em> or the <em><strong>boot process</strong></em>. The name actually comes from an old English saying, \"pulling yourself up by your <em><strong>bootstraps</strong></em>,\" which means starting something from scratch. That is exactly what a computer does — it builds itself up from nothing every time you turn it on.</p>\n<p>During <strong>booting</strong>, the computer checks its hardware (like the keyboard, mouse, memory, and hard disk), loads the operating system (such as Windows or Chrome OS), and finally presents you with a screen you can interact with.</p>\n<h2>TYPES OF BOOTING</h2>\n<p>There are two main types of booting you need to know as a JSS 1 student.</p>\n<p><strong>Cold Booting</strong></p>\n<p>Cold booting happens when you start a computer from a completely powered-off state. In other words, the computer has been completely shut down, and you press the power button to turn it on from scratch. This is the most common type of booting students experience in school.</p>\n<p>For example, when a student at Government Secondary School, Abuja, arrives in the ICT laboratory in the morning and turns on a computer that has been off since the day before, that is cold booting.</p>\n<p><strong>Warm Booting (Restarting)</strong></p>\n<p>Warm booting occurs when a computer is already on, but you restart it without completely turning it off. This is also called a restart or soft reboot. Most operating systems have a \"Restart\" option for this purpose.</p>\n<p>This type of booting is often done when a computer becomes slow, stops responding, or needs to update something. Instead of pressing the power button forcefully, you use the Restart option from the Start menu. This is the safer and preferred method.</p>\n<h2>STEPS IN BOOTING A COMPUTER</h2>\n<p>Let us walk through what actually happens, step by step, when you boot a computer. We will cover both what the computer does internally and what you as a user should do.</p>\n<p><strong>What You Should Do (User Steps)</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Step 1</strong> — Make sure the computer is properly connected. Before turning anything on, confirm that the system unit (the big box or laptop body), monitor, keyboard, and mouse are all correctly connected. In many Nigerian schools, loose cables are a common problem that can prevent a computer from starting.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 2</strong> — Connect the power supply. Ensure the power cable is plugged into a working socket. If your school or home uses a stabiliser or UPS (Uninterruptible Power Supply), switch that on first before the computer. This protects your machine from power surge damage, which is very common in areas with unstable electricity supply.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 3</strong> — Press the power button. Locate the power button on the system unit or laptop and press it once. Do not press it multiple times in a row, as this can cause problems.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 4</strong> — Wait for the system to load. You will see lights come on, you might hear a beep, and the screen will start showing information. This is the computer loading its operating system. Be patient — do not start pressing random keys.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 5</strong> — Log in if required. Once the desktop or login screen appears, enter your username and password if one is set. After that, the computer is fully booted and ready to use.</p>\n<p><strong>What the Computer Does Internally During Booting</strong></p>\n<p>While all of that is happening on the outside, a lot is going on inside the machine:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><blockquote>\n<p>The CPU (Central Processing Unit) wakes up and begins running instructions stored in a chip called the BIOS or UEFI (a kind of built-in programme that starts before anything else).</p>\n</blockquote></li>\n<li><blockquote>\n<p>The computer runs a self-test called the POST (Power-On Self-Test). This checks whether all connected hardware is working correctly. If something is wrong, the computer will beep or display an error message.</p>\n</blockquote></li>\n<li><blockquote>\n<p>After passing POST, the computer searches for an operating system to load — usually from the hard drive.</p>\n</blockquote></li>\n<li><blockquote>\n<p>The operating system (like Windows 10 or Windows 11, which is common in Nigerian schools and businesses) loads itself into the RAM.</p>\n</blockquote></li>\n<li><blockquote>\n<p>Finally, the desktop appears, and the computer is ready.</p>\n</blockquote></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Students do not need to control these internal steps — the machine handles them automatically. But it is helpful to understand what is happening so that when something goes wrong, you are not completely confused.</p>\n<h2>STEPS IN SHUTTING DOWN A COMPUTER</h2>\n<p>Shutting down a computer correctly is just as important as starting it. Many students in Nigeria simply press and hold the power button or even just unplug the system without going through the proper shutdown process. This is a dangerous habit that can corrupt files and damage the operating system over time.</p>\n<p>Here is the right way to shut down a computer:</p>\n<p><strong>Step 1</strong> — Save all open work. Before shutting down, save every document, assignment, or project you have been working on. Use Ctrl + S as a quick keyboard shortcut to save in most applications.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 2</strong> — Close all open programmes. Close your web browser, Microsoft Word, or any other application that is running. This ensures the computer shuts down cleanly without any pending tasks left open.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 3</strong> — Click on the Start button. On a Windows computer, click the Start button (the Windows icon) at the bottom left of the screen.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 4</strong> — Select \"Shut Down.\" From the power options that appear, click on \"Shut down.\" The computer will begin closing all processes and turning itself off.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 5</strong> — Wait for the screen to go dark. Do not unplug the computer or press anything while it is shutting down. Wait until the screen goes completely off and all indicator lights are out before touching anything.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 6</strong> — Switch off the stabiliser or UPS (if applicable). Only after the computer is fully off should you turn off any power backup device connected to it.</p>\n<p> <img src=\"assets/img/image65.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\"></p>\n<p><strong>RESTARTING VS. SHUTTING DOWN — WHAT IS THE DIFFERENCE?</strong></p>\n<p>A lot of students confuse these two options. Here is a simple breakdown:</p>\n<p>Shutting down means the computer turns off completely. All running programmes close, and the computer loses its current state. It starts fresh the next time you turn it on.</p>\n<p><strong>Restarting</strong> means the computer shuts down and immediately starts itself back up without any manual input. It is commonly used to apply updates, fix minor errors, or refresh the system. Think of it like taking a short nap and waking up again refreshed.</p>\n<p>In Nigerian secondary schools, teachers often ask students to restart a computer when it is running slowly rather than forcing it off with the power button. This is the recommended approach.</p>\n<h2>SAFE HANDLING OF COMPUTER DEVICES</h2>\n<p>Safe handling is all about treating your computer with care so that it lasts longer and performs better. In many schools and homes across Nigeria, computer damage is often caused by simple careless habits that could easily be avoided.</p>\n<p><strong>Physical Safety Tips</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Always carry a laptop with two hands and never swing it carelessly.</p></li>\n<li><p>Do not place heavy objects on top of a desktop computer or laptop.</p></li>\n<li><p>Keep water, food, and drinks far away from any computer device. A bottle of water spilling on a keyboard can permanently damage it.</p></li>\n<li><p>Avoid touching the screen with sharp or dirty objects.</p></li>\n<li><p>Keep the computer in a cool, well-ventilated area. Heat is one of the biggest enemies of computer hardware. In Nigerian classrooms without air conditioning, make sure windows are open for airflow.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Electrical Safety Tips</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Always use a stabiliser or surge protector. Nigeria's power supply can be unstable, with frequent surges that can fry the motherboard or other components.</p></li>\n<li><p>Do not use a computer during a thunderstorm if it is plugged into a wall socket.</p></li>\n<li><p>Never pull a power cable out roughly — always grip the plug, not the wire.</p></li>\n<li><p>If a computer starts giving off an unusual smell or smoke, switch it off immediately and report to your teacher or a technician.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Handling During Use</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Do not bang the keyboard out of frustration.</p></li>\n<li><p>Avoid pressing the power button to shut down while the computer is saving or loading something.</p></li>\n<li><p>Do not block the computer's vents — this causes overheating.</p></li>\n<li><p>Always log out properly when using a shared computer in a school lab or cyber cafe.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>PRACTICAL APPLICATIONS IN REAL LIFE</h2>\n<p>Understanding the booting and shutdown process is not just a classroom exercise. It has direct real-life uses:</p>\n<ol type=\"I\">\n<li><p>A student in Enugu who visits a cyber cafe to type and print an assignment needs to know how to properly close documents and log out before leaving the machine.</p></li>\n<li><p>A teacher in Ibadan who runs an ICT lesson needs to know how to restart a slow classroom computer without disrupting other students' work.</p></li>\n<li><p>A shop owner in Aba who uses a POS (Point of Sale) system — which is actually a small computer — needs to know that forcing it off without a proper shutdown can corrupt transaction records.</p></li>\n<li><p>Computer booting and safe shutdown is a skill that applies in homes, schools, businesses, and even hospitals where computers are used every day.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h2>CLASSROOM AND HOME ACTIVITIES</h2>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p><strong>Activity 1</strong> — Boot and Observe: With your teacher's supervision, turn on a computer from its powered-off state and observe every step that happens on the screen. Write down at least three things you notice before the desktop appears.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Activity 2</strong> — Proper Shutdown Practice: Practice shutting down a computer correctly using the Start menu. Repeat this at least three times until it feels natural.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Activity 3</strong> — Match the Type: Your teacher will write five different situations on the board (e.g., \"The computer is off and you switch it on,\" \"The computer is slow and you restart it\"). Match each situation to either \"Cold Boot\" or \"Warm Boot.\"</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Activity 4</strong> — Safety Check: Walk around your computer lab with your teacher and identify at least five things that could be unsafe — for example, loose cables, drinks near computers, or blocked vents. Report what you find.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h2>ASSESSMENT QUESTIONS</h2>\n<h2>OBJECTIVE QUESTIONS</h2>\n<p>1. The process of starting a computer from a completely powered-off state is called:</p>\n<p>A. Warm booting</p>\n<p>B. Cold booting</p>\n<p>C. Restarting</p>\n<p>D. Logging in</p>\n<p>2. Which of the following is the CORRECT first step in shutting down a computer?</p>\n<p>A. Press the power button</p>\n<p>B. Unplug the system</p>\n<p>C. Save all open work</p>\n<p>D. Remove the keyboard</p>\n<p>3. What does POST stand for in computer booting?</p>\n<p>A. Power On Self-Test</p>\n<p>B. Primary Output System Test</p>\n<p>C. Portable Operating System Transfer</p>\n<p>D. Power Output Standard Test</p>\n<p>4. Which device helps protect a computer from sudden power surges in Nigeria?</p>\n<p>A. Modem</p>\n<p>B. UPS or Stabiliser</p>\n<p>C. Scanner</p>\n<p>D. Printer</p>\n<p>5. Warm booting is best described as:</p>\n<p>A. Starting a computer for the very first time</p>\n<p>B. Turning a computer off and on using the power button only</p>\n<p>C. Restarting a computer that is already running</p>\n<p>D. Replacing a damaged operating system</p>\n\n<h2>THEORY QUESTIONS</h2>\n<p>1. Define \"booting\" and explain why it is an important process in using a computer. (3 marks)<br />\n2. Describe the correct steps you would follow to properly shut down a Windows computer. (4 marks)<br />\n3. State THREE safe handling tips that every computer user in Nigeria should follow to protect their device from damage. (3 marks)</p>\n<h2>SUMMARY</h2>\n<p>Let us quickly recap what we have covered in this lesson:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><blockquote>\n<p>Booting is the process of starting up a computer. It involves the machine running internal checks and loading the operating system before it is ready for use.</p>\n</blockquote></li>\n<li><blockquote>\n<p>There are two types of booting: cold booting (starting from off) and warm booting (restarting a running computer).</p>\n</blockquote></li>\n<li><blockquote>\n<p>During booting, the computer runs a POST (Power-On Self-Test) and loads the operating system such as Windows.</p>\n</blockquote></li>\n<li><blockquote>\n<p>To shut down properly, you should save your work, close all programmes, use the Start menu, and wait for the computer to turn off completely before unplugging anything.</p>\n</blockquote></li>\n<li><blockquote>\n<p>Safe device handling includes protecting the computer from water, heat, rough handling, and unstable power supply — all of which are especially important in the Nigerian environment.</p>\n</blockquote></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>CONCLUSION</h2>\n<p>Every skill you learn in the computer laboratory begins with knowing how to properly handle the machine in front of you. Booting a computer correctly and shutting it down safely might seem like small things, but they have a real impact on how long your device lasts and how well it performs.</p>\n<p>As a JSS 1 student, you are just beginning your journey into the world of technology. The habits you build now — saving your work before shutting down, never forcing a computer off, keeping your workspace clean and safe — will stay with you for life. Whether you become a software developer, a doctor who uses medical software, a business owner running a POS system, or a teacher in an ICT lab, these principles will always be relevant.</p>\n<p>Take care of your tools, and your tools will take care of you.</p>\n<h2>FREQUENTLY ASKED QUESTIONS (FAQ)</h2>\n<p><strong>1. What is the difference between booting and restarting a computer?</strong></p>\n<p>Booting refers to starting a computer from a completely off state (cold boot), while restarting (warm boot) means the computer shuts down briefly and starts up again on its own without being fully powered off. Restarting is often used to fix minor issues or apply updates.</p>\n<p><strong>2. Why does my computer take a long time to boot?</strong></p>\n<p>Slow booting can be caused by too many programmes set to launch at startup, a full hard drive, too little RAM, or an outdated operating system. In Nigerian school settings, it can also happen because the computers are old or have not been maintained. Informing your ICT teacher is the best step.</p>\n<p><strong>3. What happens if I just unplug my computer instead of shutting it down properly?</strong></p>\n<p>Forcefully cutting power without a proper shutdown can corrupt files, damage the operating system, and in some cases harm the hard drive. Over time, this habit can cause a computer to stop working altogether.</p>\n<p><strong>4. Is it safe to use a computer during a power outage if it is connected to a UPS?</strong></p>\n<p>Yes, a UPS (Uninterruptible Power Supply) gives you battery backup time during a power cut. However, you should use that time to save your work and shut down the computer properly rather than continuing to use it until the UPS battery runs out.</p>\n<p><strong>5. Can I eat or drink near a computer in the ICT lab?</strong></p>\n<p>No. Food crumbs can get into the keyboard and damage the keys, while liquid spills can short-circuit the entire system. Most ICT labs and examination regulations in Nigeria strictly prohibit food and drinks near computer equipment.</p>\n<p><strong>6. What does the computer check during POST (Power-On Self-Test)?</strong></p>\n<p>During POST, the computer checks whether key hardware components like the RAM, keyboard, hard drive, and processor are present and functioning correctly. If any component fails this test, the computer will display an error message or emit a series of beeps to signal the problem.</p>", "<h2>INTRODUCTION:</h2>\n<p>Picture this — your teacher asks you to submit your assignment typed and printed, but you have no idea how to even open a Word document. Sound familiar? You are not alone. Many JSS 1 students across Nigeria face this challenge every term, and that is exactly why this lesson exists.</p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/img/image66.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\"></p>\n<p>Microsoft Word, simply called MS Word, is one of the most powerful and widely used word processing software applications in the world. From schools in Lagos to offices in Abuja, from business centres in Port Harcourt to cybercafés in Enugu, MS Word is the go-to tool for typing letters, preparing reports, writing assignments, and creating professional documents of all kinds.</p>\n<p>As a JSS 1 student, learning how to use MS Word is not just about passing your Digital Technology exam — it is a skill that will serve you for the rest of your life. In today's world, being able to type, format, and print a document is almost as important as being able to read and write. So let us dive in and learn everything you need to know.</p>\n<h2>LEARNING OBJECTIVES</h2>\n<p>By the end of this lesson, students should be able to:</p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p>Define Microsoft Word and explain its purpose in everyday life</p></li>\n<li><p>Demonstrate how to open, save, and close a Word document correctly</p></li>\n<li><p>Identify the key parts of the MS Word interface</p></li>\n<li><p>Explain how to format text using bold, italic, underline, font size, and colour</p></li>\n<li><p>Describe how to insert pictures into a Word document</p></li>\n<li><p>Demonstrate how to set up a page and print a document</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h2>MICROSOFT WORD</h2>\n<p>Microsoft Word is a word processing application developed by Microsoft Corporation. It is part of the Microsoft Office Suite, which also includes Excel (for spreadsheets), PowerPoint (for presentations), and other useful programs.</p>\n<p>A word processor is simply a software program that allows you to type, edit, arrange, and print text on a computer. Think of it as a very powerful and neat typewriter — but with so many more features that make your work look professional and clean.</p>\n<p>MS Word is used to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Write letters and correspondence</p></li>\n<li><p>Type school assignments and projects</p></li>\n<li><p>Create business reports and proposals</p></li>\n<li><p>Design flyers, certificates, and newsletters</p></li>\n<li><p>Prepare application forms and CVs</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p>In Nigerian schools, MS Word is part of the Digital Technology curriculum from JSS 1, and many offices and government agencies depend on it daily for their paperwork and documentation.</p>\n<h2>PARTS OF THE MS WORD INTERFACE</h2>\n<p>Before you start using MS Word, it helps to know your way around the screen. When you open MS Word, here is what you will see:</p>\n<p><strong>The Title Bar</strong> This is the long bar at the very top of the screen. It shows the name of your current document (for example, \"Document1\") and the name of the program (Microsoft Word).</p>\n<p><strong>The Ribbon</strong> The Ribbon is the wide toolbar below the title bar. It contains tabs such as Home, Insert, Layout, References, and View. Each tab has groups of buttons and tools you can use to work on your document.</p>\n<p><strong>The Quick Access Toolbar</strong> This is a small bar at the top left corner. It has shortcuts for common actions like Save, Undo, and Redo.</p>\n<p><strong>The Document Area (Workspace)</strong> This is the big white area in the middle of the screen — this is where you type your content.</p>\n<p><strong>The Status Bar</strong> Found at the very bottom of the screen, it shows useful information like the page number, word count, and zoom level.</p>\n<p><strong>The Scroll Bar</strong> Located on the right side of the screen, it lets you move up and down through your document.</p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/img/image67.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\"><br />\n<br />\n<strong>HOW TO OPEN MS WORD</strong></p>\n<p>There are different ways to open MS Word on a computer. Here are the most common methods:</p>\n<p><strong>Method 1 — Using the Start Menu</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Click the Start button (the Windows icon at the bottom left of your screen)</p></li>\n<li><p>Type \"Microsoft Word\" in the search box</p></li>\n<li><p>Click on it when it appears in the results</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Method 2 — Using the Desktop Shortcut</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Look for the MS Word icon on your desktop (it looks like a blue \"W\")</p></li>\n<li><p>Double-click on it to open the application</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Method 3 — Opening an Existing Document</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>If you already have a saved Word file, you can simply double-click the file to open it directly in MS Word</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p>When MS Word opens, it will either show you a blank document or ask you to choose a template. For a plain, simple document, just click on \"Blank Document.\"</p>\n<h2>HOW TO CREATE AND SAVE A DOCUMENT</h2>\n<p><strong>Creating a New Document</strong></p>\n<p>Once MS Word is open, you can start typing right away in the blank white workspace. Just click anywhere on the white area and begin typing your text.</p>\n<p>To create a brand new document while MS Word is already open:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Click on File (top left corner)</p></li>\n<li><p>Select New</p></li>\n<li><p>Click on Blank Document</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Saving Your Document</strong></p>\n<p>This is one of the most important habits you must develop. Saving your work regularly prevents you from losing everything if the computer suddenly shuts down or the power goes off — which, as we all know, can happen often in Nigeria!</p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/img/image68.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\"></p>\n<p>To save a document for the first time:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Click on File</p></li>\n<li><p>Select Save As</p></li>\n<li><p>Choose where you want to save it (for example, on the Desktop or in a folder called \"My School Work\")</p></li>\n<li><p>Type a name for your document in the \"File name\" box (e.g., \"English Assignment — Week 3\")</p></li>\n<li><p>Click Save</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p><img src=\"assets/img/image69.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\"><br />\n<br />\nAfter the first save, you can quickly save any changes by pressing Ctrl + S on your keyboard. Make it a habit to press Ctrl + S every few minutes while working.</p>\n<p><strong>Saving in Different Formats</strong></p>\n<p>By default, MS Word saves files with the .docx extension (e.g., \"Assignment.docx\"). However, you can also save your file as a PDF, plain text, or older Word formats depending on what you need.</p>\n<h2>HOW TO OPEN AN EXISTING DOCUMENT</h2>\n<p>If you have already created and saved a document before, here is how to open it again:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Click on File</p></li>\n<li><p>Select Open</p></li>\n<li><p>Browse to the location where you saved your document</p></li>\n<li><p>Click on the file name</p></li>\n<li><p>Click Open</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p>You can also use the keyboard shortcut Ctrl + O to open the Open dialog box quickly.</p>\n<h2>HOW TO EDIT A DOCUMENT</h2>\n<p>Editing simply means making changes to your document after you have typed. This could involve correcting spelling mistakes, deleting words, adding new sentences, or rearranging paragraphs.</p>\n<p><strong>Basic Editing Actions:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>Click and Type</strong> — Click where you want to make a change, then start typing</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Backspace</strong> — Deletes the character to the left of your cursor</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Delete key</strong> — Deletes the character to the right of your cursor</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Ctrl + Z</strong> — Undoes your last action (very useful when you make a mistake!)</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Ctrl + Y</strong> — Redoes an action that was undone</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Ctrl + A</strong> — Selects all the text in the document</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Ctrl + C</strong> — Copies selected text</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Ctrl + V</strong> — Pastes copied text</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Ctrl + X</strong> — Cuts selected text</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Find and Replace</strong></p>\n<p>If you typed a word incorrectly many times throughout your document, you do not need to find and correct each one manually. Use:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Ctrl + H to open the Find and Replace dialog</p></li>\n<li><p>Type the wrong word in the \"Find what\" box</p></li>\n<li><p>Type the correct word in the \"Replace with\" box</p></li>\n<li><p>Click Replace All</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p><img src=\"assets/img/image70.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\"></p>\n<h2>FORMATTING TEXT IN MS WORD</h2>\n<p>Formatting is what makes your document look attractive and professional. It involves changing the appearance of your text — the font, size, colour, alignment, and style.</p>\n<p><strong>Font Type and Font Size</strong></p>\n<p>A font is a particular style of text. MS Word comes with many fonts such as Times New Roman, Arial, Calibri, and Comic Sans. To change your font:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Select (highlight) the text you want to change</p></li>\n<li><p>Click the dropdown arrow next to the font name in the Home tab</p></li>\n<li><p>Choose your preferred font</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p>To change font size:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Select the text</p></li>\n<li><p>Click the font size box (next to the font name)</p></li>\n<li><p>Type a number or select one from the list (e.g., 12 for body text, 14 or 16 for headings)</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Bold, Italic, and Underline</strong></p>\n<p>These are the most basic formatting tools and they are used to give emphasis to text.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>Bold (Ctrl + B)</strong> — Makes text appear thicker and heavier. Used for headings or important words.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Italic (Ctrl + I)</strong> — Makes text slant to the right. Used for book titles, foreign words, or emphasis.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Underline (Ctrl + U)</strong> — Draws a line under the text. Often used for titles or key terms.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p>You can also combine them — for example, a word can be both bold and underlined at the same time.</p>\n<p><strong>Text Colour</strong></p>\n<p>To change the colour of your text:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Select the text</p></li>\n<li><p>Click the small arrow beside the Font Color button (looks like the letter A with a colour bar under it) in the Home tab</p></li>\n<li><p>Choose your preferred colour from the palette</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Text Alignment</strong></p>\n<p>Alignment controls how your text lines up on the page:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>Align Left (Ctrl + L)</strong> — Text lines up on the left side (this is the default)</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Center (Ctrl + E)</strong> — Text is centered on the page. Used for headings and titles</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Align Right (Ctrl + R)</strong> — Text lines up on the right side</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Justify (Ctrl + J)</strong> — Text stretches to fill the full width of the page, like in newspapers</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Line Spacing</strong></p>\n<p>Line spacing controls the space between lines of text. To change it:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Select your text</p></li>\n<li><p>Click on the Line and Paragraph Spacing button in the Home tab</p></li>\n<li><p>Choose 1.0, 1.5, 2.0, etc. depending on how much space you want</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>INSERTING PICTURES INTO A WORD DOCUMENT</h2>\n<p>A picture can make your document more interesting and easier to understand. Imagine a student in JSS 1 preparing a project on the water cycle — adding a diagram makes the work far more impressive than text alone.</p>\n<p><strong>How to Insert a Picture from Your Computer</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Click where you want the image to appear in your document</p></li>\n<li><p>Click on the Insert tab on the Ribbon</p></li>\n<li><p>Click on Pictures</p></li>\n<li><p>Select This Device (or From File on older versions)</p></li>\n<li><p>Browse to the folder where your image is saved</p></li>\n<li><p>Click on the image, then click Insert</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>How to Insert an Online Picture</strong></p>\n<p>If your computer is connected to the internet:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Click on Insert</p></li>\n<li><p>Click on Online Pictures</p></li>\n<li><p>Type a description of the image you want (e.g., \"map of Nigeria\")</p></li>\n<li><p>Press Enter and choose from the results shown</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Resizing and Positioning a Picture</strong></p>\n<p>Once your image is inserted:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Click on it to select it. Small squares (called handles) will appear around the edges</p></li>\n<li><p>Drag any corner handle to make the image bigger or smaller</p></li>\n<li><p>Click and drag the image to move it to a different position on the page</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p>You can also right-click the image and select \"Wrap Text\" to control how the surrounding text flows around the picture.</p>\n<h2>PAGE SETUP IN MS WORD</h2>\n<p>Page setup involves configuring how your document page looks before you print or share it.</p>\n<p><strong>Paper Size</strong></p>\n<p>In Nigeria, most printed documents use either A4 paper (the standard size used in most offices and schools) or Letter size. To set your paper size:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Click on the Layout tab</p></li>\n<li><p>Click on Size</p></li>\n<li><p>Select A4 or your preferred size</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Page Orientation</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>Portrait</strong> — The page is taller than it is wide (this is the default and most common)</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Landscape</strong> — The page is wider than it is tall (useful for wide tables and charts)</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p>To change orientation:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Click on Layout</p></li>\n<li><p>Click on Orientation</p></li>\n<li><p>Choose Portrait or Landscape</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Page Margins</strong></p>\n<p>Margins are the empty spaces around the edges of the page. They help your document look neat.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Click on Layout</p></li>\n<li><p>Click on Margins</p></li>\n<li><p>Choose Normal (2.54 cm on all sides), Narrow, Wide, or Custom Margins</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>PRINTING YOUR DOCUMENT</h2>\n<p>When your document is ready, you can print it. Make sure a printer is connected and has paper loaded before you begin.</p>\n<p><strong>Steps to Print:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Click on File</p></li>\n<li><p>Click on Print</p></li>\n<li><p>A print preview will appear on the right side so you can see how the document will look when printed</p></li>\n<li><p>Choose the number of copies you want</p></li>\n<li><p>Select the printer from the dropdown if more than one is available</p></li>\n<li><p>Click the Print button</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p><img src=\"assets/img/image71.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Illustration\"></p>\n<p><strong>Print Settings to Know:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>All Pages</strong> — Prints the entire document</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Current Page</strong> — Prints only the page you are currently on</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Custom Range</strong> — Lets you print specific pages (e.g., pages 2 to 5)</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p>You can also use the keyboard shortcut Ctrl + P to open the print dialog quickly.</p>\n<h2>PRACTICAL APPLICATIONS (REAL-LIFE NIGERIAN EXAMPLES)</h2>\n<p>Let us look at how MS Word is used in real life situations that you can relate to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>School Assignments</strong> — Chidi, a JSS 1 student in Onitsha, uses MS Word to type and print his Biology project on mammals. His teacher gives him extra marks because the work is neatly formatted.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Business Letters</strong> — Mr. Adebayo, a shop owner in Ibadan, uses MS Word to write letters to his suppliers requesting new stock.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Church Bulletins and Programmes</strong> — Many churches in Nigeria type their Sunday service bulletins in MS Word, format them nicely, and print them out for members.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Government Documents</strong> — Local government offices in states like Kano, Rivers, and Cross River use MS Word to type official letters, memos, and reports.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>School Notices</strong> — School administrators use MS Word to type announcements, timetables, and examination schedules.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>ADVANTAGES OF MS WORD</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Easy to type, edit, and correct mistakes</p></li>\n<li><p>Documents look clean and professional</p></li>\n<li><p>You can save and retrieve documents anytime</p></li>\n<li><p>Files can be shared via email, USB drive, or WhatsApp</p></li>\n<li><p>Supports spelling and grammar checks</p></li>\n<li><p>Compatible with most computers and printers</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>DISADVANTAGES OF MS WORD</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Requires electricity and a computer to use</p></li>\n<li><p>Some advanced features can be confusing for beginners</p></li>\n<li><p>The full version (Microsoft Office) requires purchase or subscription</p></li>\n<li><p>Files can be lost if not properly saved or backed up</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>SAFETY AND ETHICAL CONSIDERATIONS</h2>\n<p>Using MS Word responsibly means more than just knowing the technical steps. Here are a few things to keep in mind:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>Do not copy and paste another person's work</strong> and present it as your own. This is called plagiarism and is considered dishonest and unethical.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Save your work regularly</strong> to avoid losing it due to power failure.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Always ask for permission</strong> before printing with a school or office printer.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Handle the computer with care</strong> — do not eat or drink near the keyboard and mouse.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Log out or lock the computer</strong> when you are done, especially in a school computer lab. Never leave your personal documents open for others to see or edit.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>CLASSROOM AND HOME ACTIVITIES</h2>\n<p><strong>Activity 1 — Typing Practice</strong> Open MS Word, create a new document, and type a short paragraph about your school. Apply bold to your school name, centre-align the title, and save the file as \"My School.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Activity 2 — Formatting Challenge</strong> Type the following sentence five times: \"Digital Technology is an important subject.\" Each time, change the font type, size, colour, and alignment. Save your work.</p>\n<p><strong>Activity 3 — Insert and Resize</strong> Create a document about your favourite animal. Type at least two paragraphs about it, then insert a picture of the animal. Resize the image and position it neatly beside the text.</p>\n<p><strong>Activity 4 — Print Setup</strong> Open any saved document. Go to Page Setup and change the orientation to Landscape. Then set the margins to Narrow. Preview how the page looks and share what you observe with your classmates.</p>\n<h2>ASSESSMENT QUESTIONS</h2>\n<p><strong>Section A — Objective Questions</strong></p>\n<p>1. What does MS Word stand for?</p>\n<p>A. Microsoft Works</p>\n<p>B. Microsoft Word Processing</p>\n<p>C. Microsoft Word</p>\n<p>D. Master Software Word</p>\n<p>2. Which keyboard shortcut is used to save a document quickly?</p>\n<p>A. Ctrl + P</p>\n<p>B. Ctrl + S</p>\n<p>C. Ctrl + Z</p>\n<p>D. Ctrl + A</p>\n<p>3. Which of the following is NOT a text formatting option in MS Word?</p>\n<p>A. Bold</p>\n<p>B. Italic</p>\n<p>C. Zoom</p>\n<p>D. Underline</p>\n<p>4. The white area in the middle of the MS Word screen where you type your content is called the______.</p>\n<p>A. Status bar</p>\n<p>B. Ribbon</p>\n<p>C. Document area</p>\n<p>D. Title bar</p>\n<p>5. To insert a picture into a Word document, which tab do you click first?</p>\n<p>A. Home</p>\n<p>B. View</p>\n<p>C. Insert</p>\n<p>D. Layout</p>\n<p><strong>Section B — Theory Questions</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p>Explain THREE differences between saving a document using \"Save\" and \"Save As\" in MS Word.</p></li>\n<li><p>Describe the steps you would follow to format a paragraph heading in MS Word. Your answer should include how to change the font, apply bold, and center-align the text.</p></li>\n<li><p>Your school teacher has asked you to print only pages 3 to 7 of a 10-page document. Explain the steps you would take to achieve this in MS Word.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h2>SUMMARY</h2>\n<p>In this lesson, we covered the following key points:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>MS Word is a word processing application used to create, edit, format, and print text documents</p></li>\n<li><p>The MS Word interface includes the Title Bar, Ribbon, Document Area, Status Bar, and Scroll Bar</p></li>\n<li><p>To open MS Word, you can use the Start menu or desktop shortcut</p></li>\n<li><p>Documents are created in the blank workspace and saved using File &gt; Save As or Ctrl + S</p></li>\n<li><p>Text formatting options include font type, font size, bold, italic, underline, colour, and alignment</p></li>\n<li><p>Pictures can be inserted from your computer or online using the Insert tab</p></li>\n<li><p>Page setup options include paper size, orientation (portrait or landscape), and margins</p></li>\n<li><p>Documents are printed using File &gt; Print or Ctrl + P</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>CONCLUSION</h2>\n<p>Microsoft Word is truly one of the most important software tools you will ever learn to use. Whether you are a student writing an assignment, a trader writing a business letter, or a teacher preparing lesson notes, MS Word makes the job easier, faster, and much more professional.</p>\n<p>As a JSS 1 student, mastering the basics of MS Word — opening and saving documents, formatting your text, inserting images, setting up your page, and printing — gives you a solid foundation that will help you throughout your secondary school education and beyond. With regular practice, you will become more confident and eventually discover even more powerful features that MS Word has to offer.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember</strong>: the more you practice, the better you get. So open that computer, launch MS Word, and start typing!</p>\n<h2>FREQUENTLY ASKED QUESTIONS (FAQ)</h2>\n<p><strong>1. What is MS Word used for?</strong> </p>\n<p>MS Word is used to type, edit, format, and print text documents. It is commonly used for school assignments, business letters, reports, flyers, and many other forms of written communication.</p>\n<p><strong>2. How do I save a Word document so I do not lose my work?</strong> </p>\n<p>You can save your document by clicking File &gt; Save As the first time, and after that, pressing Ctrl + S regularly to save any new changes. It is a good idea to save every few minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>3. Can I use MS Word without the internet?</strong> <br />\nYes! MS Word works perfectly without an internet connection. You only need the internet if you want to use features like online pictures or cloud saving through OneDrive.</p>\n<p><strong>4. What is the difference between Bold and Italic in MS Word?</strong> <br />\nBold makes your text thicker and heavier (like <strong>this</strong>), while Italic makes your text slant slightly to the right (like <em>this</em>). Both are used to add emphasis to words or phrases in a document.</p>\n<p><strong>5. How do I fix a mistake I made while typing in MS Word?</strong> </p>\n<p>You can press the Backspace key to delete characters before your cursor, or press the Delete key to remove characters after your cursor. You can also press Ctrl + Z to undo your last action.</p>\n<p><strong>6. Can I change the size of a picture after inserting it into MS Word?</strong> <br />\nYes. Once a picture is inserted, click on it to select it. Small square handles will appear around its edges. Drag any corner handle inward to make it smaller or outward to make it larger. Always use a corner handle to keep the image proportions balanced.</p>"];
window.SCHEME_HTML="<table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Week</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Topic</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Breakdown (Subtopics)</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Page</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1.</td>\n<td>Introduction to Digital Technologies</td>\n<td><p>• Meaning of digital technology</p>\n<p>• Importance in modern life</p>\n<p>• Examples of digital devices</p></td>\n<td>2-8</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2.</td>\n<td>Historical Development of Digital Devices</td>\n<td><p>• Early calculating tools (Abacus, Napier’s Bones)</p>\n<p>• Generations of computers</p>\n<p>• Evolution from analog to digital devices</p>\n<p>• Modern digital trends</p></td>\n<td>9-17</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3.</td>\n<td>Career Opportunities in Information Technology</td>\n<td><p>• Careers in ICT: software, hardware, networking, data</p>\n<p>• Emerging digital careers (AI, cybersecurity, robotics)</p>\n<p>• Skills needed for IT careers</p></td>\n<td>18-25</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4.</td>\n<td>Basic Computer Hardware Components I</td>\n<td><p>• Meaning of hardware</p>\n<p>• Input and output devices</p>\n<p>• Functions and identification of devices</p>\n<p>• Simple maintenance practices</p></td>\n<td>26-32</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5.</td>\n<td><strong>MID-TERM TEST</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Assessment of Weeks 1–4</strong></td>\n<td><strong>-</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6.</td>\n<td>Basic Computer Hardware Components II: Storage Units</td>\n<td><p>• Primary and secondary storage</p>\n<p>• Examples: RAM, ROM, hard disk, flash drive</p>\n<p>• Storage measurements (bit, byte, MB, GB, TB)</p>\n<p>• Importance of data storage</p></td>\n<td>33-43</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7.</td>\n<td><strong>MID-TERM BREAK</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Holiday/Rest</strong></td>\n<td><strong>-</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8.</td>\n<td>Basic Software Concepts</td>\n<td><p>• Meaning of software</p>\n<p>• System software and application software</p>\n<p>• Examples: OS, Word processors, browsers</p>\n<p>• Functions of an operating system</p></td>\n<td>44-56</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9.</td>\n<td>Computer Booting and Shutdown Processes</td>\n<td><p>• Meaning of booting</p>\n<p>• Steps in booting and shutting down</p>\n<p>• Safe handling of devices</p></td>\n<td>57-62</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10.</td>\n<td>MS Word (Introduction)</td>\n<td><p>• Opening, saving, and editing documents</p>\n<p>• Formatting text and inserting pictures</p>\n<p>• Page setup and printing basics</p></td>\n<td>63-73</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>11.</td>\n<td><strong>REVISION</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Review of all topics covered</strong></td>\n<td><strong>-</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>12.</td>\n<td><strong>EXAMINATION</strong></td>\n<td><strong>End of Term Assessment</strong></td>\n<td><strong>-</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>13.</td>\n<td><strong>CLOSING/VACATION</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Term ends</strong></td>\n<td><strong>-</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>";
